WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:06.630
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.030
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:09.610
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:10.870
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.010
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

6
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.530
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

7
00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.710
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

8
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.730
We're The Office Lady 6.0.

9
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.160
Hello there.

10
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.420
Hi, everyone.

11
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.820
We have such a big episode of Office Lady 6.0 today because, you know,

12
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.520
on Monday, it was the 20th anniversary of The Office.

13
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.260
And we thought, well, we have to celebrate it today.

14
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.120
This is a big milestone.

15
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.740
A lot of you wrote us about this.

16
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.040
Bethany C.

17
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.780
from Denver, Colorado said,

18
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:51.640
Hello, ladies.

19
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.480
The first episode of The Office aired in the U.S.

20
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.860
on March 24, 2005,

21
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.040
which means this year will mark 20 years.

22
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.920
Is there a plan to celebrate the big anniversary?

23
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.100
And Brianna S.

24
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.620
from Athens, Ohio said,

25
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:12.520
Something I would be interested in as possible episodes now that you're doing character breakdowns

26
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.120
would be a cast or crew breakdown.

27
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.080
For example, how about all about Greg Daniels?

28
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.700
Well, Bethany and Brianna, you are in luck.

29
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:29.160
Today, we are celebrating the 20th anniversary of The Office with special guest, Greg Daniels.

30
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.980
It was so wonderful talking with Greg.

31
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.740
We had such a great conversation about 20 years of The Office.

32
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.500
Not only did we ask him about how he came to run the show,

33
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.280
but we also reflect on so many wonderful memories.

34
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:44.200
Yeah.

35
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.440
As this anniversary was approaching, Angela and I,

36
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:53.800
we both went back and we rewatched the pilot episode of The Office.

37
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.640
It was so interesting.

38
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.600
We were like the people who go back and start from the beginning in a way.

39
00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:00.220
I know.

40
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.480
I watched the original broadcast and then I went and watched the super fan version because

41
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:06.320
I was curious.

42
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.440
I did the same thing and we talked with Greg about it.

43
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.900
And you know, guys, I went back into some of my, well, I guess it's not my digital clutter.

44
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.780
It's my actual clutter.

45
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.720
I went into my bin of photos.

46
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.240
I found the photos from the pilot, from the party we went to after the pilot.

47
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.940
Some of those, Jenna, we shared in our book, The Office BFFs.

48
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.880
Some we used in a reel that Josh made for us to celebrate the 20th anniversary.

49
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.560
Some I didn't put in either.

50
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.000
I'm going to share them in stories.

51
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.340
But then, you guys, are you ready for this?

52
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.300
I went to my journal.

53
00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.200
That is titled, what's it called, Jenna?

54
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.160
Office Early Days.

55
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:48.720
Uh-huh.

56
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.060
I have a journal entry that I can't wait to share with you guys.

57
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.940
It is from our very first day of filming.

58
00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.960
That is nuts to me.

59
00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.540
Are you ready?

60
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.300
You told me you found this and I can't wait.

61
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.160
Well, I've been holding on to this for a while because I found it after we had already broke

62
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.180
down the pilot and I was like, oh, dang it.

63
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.520
So here we go.

64
00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.000
September 20th, 2004, 7.30 a.m.

65
00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.780
First day of shooting of The Office.

66
00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.260
That is written in blue ink.

67
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.880
That means you wrote this on the set of The Office.

68
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:23.780
Oh, yeah.

69
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.120
It's going to be very clear.

70
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:25.580
Okay.

71
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.320
So at 7.30 a.m.

72
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.640
I wrote first day of shooting of The Office.

73
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.040
That's in blue ink.

74
00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.460
Next line is in black ink.

75
00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.620
This is the best job ever.

76
00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.400
Next line is in red ink.

77
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.080
I have all of these pins on my desk, so I'm trying them all out.

78
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.380
Oh, my gosh.

79
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:48.780
Okay.

80
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.620
Next line is in black ink.

81
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.920
My call time was 5 a.m.

82
00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.360
I'm so sleepy, exclamation point.

83
00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.600
I didn't even really fall asleep in the first place.

84
00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.340
12.40 p.m.

85
00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:02.340
in red ink.

86
00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.440
New timestamp, new ink.

87
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:04.780
Uh-huh.

88
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.560
I'm so sleepy, but I'm having the time of my life.

89
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.540
4.35 p.m.

90
00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:11.760
in black ink.

91
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.080
I'm absolutely so punchy.

92
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.220
Drew a black heart.

93
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:18.220
Okay.

94
00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.800
Almost asleep with my eyes open.

95
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.780
Oh, just got flowers from my agent.

96
00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.020
Congrats, et cetera.

97
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.020
Very nice.

98
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:28.940
Okay.

99
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.320
Next thing.

100
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:31.520
Blue ink.

101
00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.460
Here is the letterhead from our fake company.

102
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.740
Also one of my call sheets.

103
00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.040
And then you taped it inside?

104
00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:39.580
Mm-hmm.

105
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.640
Oh, stop it.

106
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:41.480
It's so cute.

107
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:42.920
All right.

108
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:44.080
Next entry.

109
00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:45.100
In black ink.

110
00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.260
Gosh, I so want this show to be a success.

111
00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:50.620
Next entry.

112
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.060
I'm already looking forward to Christmas.

113
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:53.980
Is that nuts?

114
00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.980
It's my favorite time of the year.

115
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:57.580
I can't wait to decorate.

116
00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:58.720
Wow.

117
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.300
That was a hard pivot.

118
00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.040
I know.

119
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:01.800
I know.

120
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.360
It's what was on your mind.

121
00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:03.900
Okay.

122
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:04.700
Are you ready?

123
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.000
Final entry from the first day.

124
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.560
My pantyhose are cutting me in half.

125
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.960
How do women wear these all the time?

126
00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:12.700
Seriously?

127
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.400
Question mark, exclamation point.

128
00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.700
I want to ditch them, exclamation point.

129
00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:19.820
And you did.

130
00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:20.760
And I did.

131
00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.200
There you go.

132
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.600
First day on the set of The Office.

133
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.380
That's pretty amazing.

134
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.700
Sam, I can't believe you didn't pop on to give me a hard time about all the different

135
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.300
color ink.

136
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.340
I didn't want to interrupt, but it was killing me.

137
00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.700
Why was I so excited about all my different pens?

138
00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:38.280
I don't know.

139
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.060
I like to think it's your different moods.

140
00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.460
Well, Allison Jones found something in her clutter as well, and she sent it to me out

141
00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:45.920
of the blue.

142
00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.260
She found the casting descriptions for the main caSt.

143
00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:56.960
These were written by Greg Daniels, and I thought it would be so fun to read them.

144
00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.340
I can't wait to hear these.

145
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.820
I knew you got them, but I haven't heard them yet.

146
00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:05.940
And I thought what would be fun is as you read one, I would play some of the audition

147
00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.140
tapes that are online, and we can go back and forth.

148
00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:10.200
I love it.

149
00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.240
All right, let's start with Michael Scott.

150
00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.680
Here is how he is described.

151
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.000
34 to 44.

152
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.260
Michael Scott is the manager of The Office and the boastful, unreliable narrator of the

153
00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:23.860
documentary.

154
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:30.460
He is a legend in his own mind who thinks he is a comic genius, fountain of business wisdom,

155
00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.580
and his employee's cool friend.

156
00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.920
He believes in his version of reality with the sincere enthusiasm of a nine-year-old child

157
00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.400
thinking he can do karate.

158
00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.060
However, the documentary reveals the truth.

159
00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:52.460
He is a buffoon, a pathetic mid-level bureaucrat overdue for a midlife crisis, whom decent people

160
00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:58.200
pity as a, quote, sad little man when his inappropriate behavior hasn't appalled them

161
00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.140
into silence.

162
00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:05.380
Horribly overconfident, he is a train wreck of bad leadership characteristics only redeemed

163
00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.780
a bit by his childish enthusiasm.

164
00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:15.220
Despite continual proofs that he's an ass, he clings shamelessly to his deluded self-image

165
00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.440
like a shipwreck survivor clinging to a scrap of wood.

166
00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:25.160
And then it says, we need an actor who can play a juicy comic character, someone with an

167
00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:31.400
expressive face to get a laugh on a smug look, someone with the heart of a nine-year-old, but

168
00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:37.660
who plays between 34 and 44, someone whose face and physique do not command.

169
00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:45.200
And natural respect, i.e. not buff and handsome, boyish, not rugged, capable of high-spirited,

170
00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.620
sunny energy, as well as small, specific acting.

171
00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:49.940
Wow.

172
00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.080
That's so much information.

173
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:52.680
Yeah.

174
00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:56.460
Well, here is Steve reading for Michael.

175
00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.720
This is just a portion of his audition, but let's hear it.

176
00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.620
Actually, people say I am the best boss they've ever had.

177
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.600
The world's greatest boss given to me.

178
00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:11.300
They go, Michael, we have never worked in a place like this before.

179
00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.320
I mean, you are a riot.

180
00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.060
You get the best out of us.

181
00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.800
And I say, well, they're gay, sirrah, sirrah.

182
00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:23.480
You know?

183
00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.060
And if it's true, awesome.

184
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.630
What is gay, sirrah, sirrah?

185
00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:37.690
Well, it's kind of a non-sequitur, I've been told.

186
00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:41.150
It's kind of, you know, kind of filler words.

187
00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:42.390
Have you ever heard of filler words?

188
00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:42.770
Yes.

189
00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:46.550
It's kind of like that, you know, que, sirrah, sirrah.

190
00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:58.760
Um, if I had to define what it meant, I'd say, um, what, what context did I use it, Jessen?

191
00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.830
That's so great.

192
00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:06.010
And I love that you can hear Ken Coaffes.

193
00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:06.610
Yes.

194
00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:11.590
Well, what's so crazy about it is that it starts and it just sounds like an episode of The Office.

195
00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:15.510
Like, it just sounds like he's, it doesn't sound like an audition at all.

196
00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:16.010
I know.

197
00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:23.390
But then you see how our auditions went, which is that we would do the prepared material and then Ken would just throw a question at us.

198
00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:24.790
Like, what's case for Ross?

199
00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:25.670
Yeah, exactly.

200
00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:26.690
It's a filler word.

201
00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:27.030
Yeah.

202
00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.070
It's just one of those filler words.

203
00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:29.290
So great.

204
00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.750
Are you ready to hear about Dwight Schrute?

205
00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:32.290
Yes.

206
00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.570
Here is how Greg described him in the breakdown.

207
00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:38.130
Dwight is the team leader and Michael Scott's sidekick.

208
00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:49.570
He really admires Michael Scott, although it is unclear if this is due to Scott's personality or Dwight's officious inclination to look up to whoever is above him in the hierarchy.

209
00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:59.570
Dwight is obsessed with survival, personal security tactics, and other grandiose nerd action fantasies, probably because he got his ass kicked a lot as a kid.

210
00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:05.090
A volunteer policeman on the weekends, he takes any excuse to go on a power trip in the office.

211
00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:10.210
Yet his survival training appears to be more Gilligan's Island than Green Beret's.

212
00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:16.350
Although aggressively horny, he has no idea how to behave with women.

213
00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:25.650
His unpleasant social habits and annoying personalities suggest an unsocialized loner, a sort of caliban or golem.

214
00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.770
If stuck in an elevator, he would probably start drinking his own urine after 10 minutes.

215
00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:39.430
I have just like side note, just this idea that Dwight gets stuck in an elevator goes all the way back to the breakdown.

216
00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:41.530
I love that.

217
00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.770
Like how Dwight would behave in an elevator.

218
00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:45.190
All right.

219
00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:45.810
Going on.

220
00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:47.130
He said,

221
00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:05.070
We need someone who can be believable as a geek.

222
00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:11.990
Someone who has no desire to be likable or please an audience except through total identification with his character.

223
00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:13.790
This is so Rain.

224
00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:14.510
I know.

225
00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:18.730
I have to think the minute Rain walked in the room, they were like, well, crap, that's Dwight.

226
00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:23.430
Someone who can seem reasonable to himself while saying insane things.

227
00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:27.410
Who understands the comedy of playing it straight.

228
00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:29.850
Late 20s to 30s.

229
00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:31.350
Oh my gosh.

230
00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:32.190
Okay.

231
00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.950
Well, now we have to hear Rain auditioning for Dwight.

232
00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:39.210
Urine is sterile.

233
00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:40.630
Okay.

234
00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:42.710
That's a fact.

235
00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:43.550
Did you know that?

236
00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:47.550
When you are in the field, you can clean a wound by whizzing on it.

237
00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:49.170
True.

238
00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:53.670
It is in the first aid guidebook from the American Red Cross Association.

239
00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:57.030
So it's totally unfair for me to get in trouble for whizzing in the sink.

240
00:11:59.350 --> 00:11:59.830
Amazing.

241
00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.790
Well, I know there's so much more footage, but that's what you can pull from online.

242
00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:08.250
And it's, you just, you hear him immediately as Dwight.

243
00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:13.610
I felt like as all of us, and this was true when I went back just now and re-watched the pilot,

244
00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:20.290
I think Rain as Dwight was the most fully realized of all of us from day one.

245
00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:20.490
Day one.

246
00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:20.770
Yeah.

247
00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:21.850
All right.

248
00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:25.090
Next up is the breakdown for Jim Nelson.

249
00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:29.070
The original name was Jim Nelson.

250
00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:34.170
Jim is a sales rep in the office who has to share a workspace with Dwight.

251
00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:39.650
He is an ordinary, decent person with good taste, leading a life of quiet desperation.

252
00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:45.050
He likes people, is a good listener, and wanted to be a psychologiSt.

253
00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:46.690
Hmm.

254
00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:47.450
Wow.

255
00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:54.670
His clever sarcasm and takes to the camera are little defense against the vulgarity that surrounds him,

256
00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:57.530
although they make Pam the receptionist laugh.

257
00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:01.110
You wish he would be more assertive in love and at work.

258
00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:08.410
After playing with Pam, his chief enjoyment in the office is using his superior social and emotional skills to prank Dwight.

259
00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:15.470
Although you get the sense that when he indulges in his immature impulses, he is letting the environment defeat him.

260
00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:23.430
We need someone likable, around 30, who can get laughs by raising an eyebrow or doing a take to the camera.

261
00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:29.890
He needs to be pleasant-looking enough for you to root for him to get the girl without being a hunk in any way.

262
00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:35.730
Although hidden by his ordinariness and bad haircut, Jim is the romantic lead.

263
00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.430
They wrote in bad haircut?

264
00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:37.810
Yeah.

265
00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:39.410
All right.

266
00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:41.830
Here is John auditioning for Jim.

267
00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:54.990
I'm a sales rep, which means that my job is to speak to clients on the phone about quantity, type of paper,

268
00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:02.130
whether we can supply it to them, whether they can pay for it.

269
00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:14.630
I mean, that just sounds like it's from the show, honestly.

270
00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:15.230
I know.

271
00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:16.990
That was an audition tape.

272
00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:18.390
All right.

273
00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.750
Finally, here is Pam Beasley's breakdown.

274
00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:25.770
26 to 29 was the age range.

275
00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:27.290
And it says,

276
00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:30.150
Pam is the receptionist and Jim's friend.

277
00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:32.930
Pam is decent, reasonable, and friendly.

278
00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:37.350
She has the manner of a nice kindergarten teacher or a future mom.

279
00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:40.570
She is an ordinary woman with a sense of humor.

280
00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:45.150
She allows her loudish boss and fiancé to push her around some,

281
00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:49.230
but she can exhibit flashes of working-class toughness in protecting her friends.

282
00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:55.550
She's not cynical or a smartass, although her way of disagreeing is a gentle sarcasm.

283
00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:59.310
She's not arrogant or glamorous or overtly sexy,

284
00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:02.490
but she is cute compared to the other office workers,

285
00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:07.970
and she loves to play with Jim, who understands her better than Roy, her fiancé.

286
00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:13.310
Jim and Pam probably would not have met without being thrown together in the office,

287
00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:15.370
but they have become true friends,

288
00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:18.470
and their flirting is more serious than they acknowledge.

289
00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:22.490
Pam needs to be soft and kind and vulnerable.

290
00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:25.590
Pretty, too, but definitely not a head-turner.

291
00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.850
More of a likable, accessible pretty.

292
00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:32.970
A working-world, girl-next-door type who can deliver sarcasm with a light touch,

293
00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:35.390
yet a touch of the tragic waif,

294
00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:37.890
Pam is the other romantic lead.

295
00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:40.930
Oh, man, when you watch your audition tape,

296
00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:42.110
when you watch the pilot,

297
00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:44.930
Jenna, you got Pam right away.

298
00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:46.510
You just did.

299
00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:49.490
And let's hear you audition for Pam.

300
00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:51.490
I hope they get rid of me,

301
00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:55.410
because then I might actually get off my ass and do something,

302
00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:59.090
because I don't think it's many little girls' dreams to grow up and be a receptioniSt.

303
00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:01.690
And I don't know what I'm going to do,

304
00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:04.310
but whatever it is, it's got to be a career move,

305
00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:06.370
not just another arbitrary job.

306
00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:11.770
And Jim's advice was that it's better to be at the bottom of a ladder that you want to climb

307
00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:13.310
than halfway up when you don't.

308
00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:16.710
Yeah, I remember doing that.

309
00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:20.110
She's not quoting Roy, even in the audition.

310
00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:21.290
Yeah, that's right.

311
00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:28.330
All right, next up, I have two batches of shorter clips of multiple characters auditioning.

312
00:16:28.450 --> 00:16:36.070
We have BJ for Ryan, Craig for Daryl, David for Roy, Dave for Todd, and Melora for Jan.

313
00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:37.390
Let's hear them.

314
00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:39.510
Okay, I'm into foreign filMs.

315
00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:42.570
I read The New Yorker, love Shakespeare.

316
00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:45.990
Did you ever see the Dunder Mifflin flag out in the parking lot?

317
00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:47.910
Isn't it inspiring?

318
00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.790
I think I'm wanting it on my coffin.

319
00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:53.350
Hey, baby, you got it?

320
00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:56.690
Yeah, would you mind if I went up for a drink with these guys?

321
00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:01.450
No, no, let's go home.

322
00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:04.710
It's a gimmick, I think, really.

323
00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:08.870
You know, casual Friday, one day you let your hair down.

324
00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:09.610
That's pathetic.

325
00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:12.050
Michael, go on.

326
00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:13.710
Can we not talk about downsizing?

327
00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:15.310
Oh, we have to sooner or later.

328
00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:20.410
Yes, but right now what we have to decide is do you fold Stanford people into this branch or vice versa?

329
00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:24.850
Those are just quick little snippets of each person auditioning.

330
00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:26.750
And then what's the other batch?

331
00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:28.090
Do you have the rest of you guys?

332
00:17:28.090 --> 00:17:36.990
I have Brian for Kevin, Oscar for Oscar, Angela for Pam, Leslie for Stanley, and Kate for Meredith.

333
00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:39.690
What did you watch on TV last night?

334
00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:41.430
I didn't, I rented a movie.

335
00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:46.310
I watched that special victim SVU.

336
00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:47.530
Never seen that.

337
00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:50.290
Friggin' repeat.

338
00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:51.670
Well, that sucks.

339
00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.750
Well, that sucks.

340
00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:54.570
Not for me.

341
00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:56.670
I hadn't seen it.

342
00:17:57.170 --> 00:17:58.530
It is a question of truSt.

343
00:17:58.710 --> 00:17:59.410
It's communication.

344
00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:01.770
Do you trust me?

345
00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:02.310
Do you trust me?

346
00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:02.730
Yes or no?

347
00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:04.090
Yes.

348
00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:05.730
I trust you.

349
00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.190
I like that.

350
00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:13.070
Because it's not often that you get something that's both romantic and thrifty.

351
00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:15.150
So there's that going.

352
00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:19.490
I said if Corpora wants to come here and interfere, you ain't messing with my chillin'.

353
00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:20.690
I am if anyone's going to.

354
00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:24.010
Yeah, but Michael, what if they do downsize here?

355
00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:25.290
Not going to happen.

356
00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:27.030
It could be out of your hands, Michael.

357
00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:30.230
Was there anything that you wanted to add to the agenda?

358
00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:31.950
That was it?

359
00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:33.370
Kate just got one line?

360
00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:36.190
Is there anything you wanted to add to the agenda?

361
00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:37.850
Well, they're just little clips.

362
00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:39.230
Our auditions were much longer.

363
00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:42.070
We improvised a whole bunch and did all these different takes.

364
00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.250
But that's what we have access to.

365
00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:48.370
And I don't have me auditioning for Angela Martin.

366
00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:52.310
We might have to hit up Dave Rogers for that when we do all about Angela Martin.

367
00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:53.730
Oh, we should.

368
00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:55.970
Let's see if we can get a hold of that.

369
00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:56.450
I know.

370
00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:58.930
That would be going into the archives for sure.

371
00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:00.290
Well, listen, everyone.

372
00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:01.350
We're going to take a break.

373
00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:06.390
And when we come back, we have a fantastic interview with showrunner Greg Daniels.

374
00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:21.100
Greg Daniels.

375
00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:22.580
Welcome, Greg.

376
00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:23.340
Thank you.

377
00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:23.740
Here you are.

378
00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:29.120
You were on the regular Office Ladies, and now you're on Office Ladies 6.0.

379
00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:31.920
How did you jump to 6.0 instead of 2.0?

380
00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.360
Greg, Greg, this is a deep cut.

381
00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.080
Oh, that's a joke from the show.

382
00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:36.580
Yes.

383
00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:36.900
Yes.

384
00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:39.480
This interview is not going well.

385
00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:41.540
So wait, what show is this?

386
00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:43.480
I think I saw this.

387
00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:50.080
In Hot Girl, Michael says that Katie is Pam 6.0.

388
00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:50.720
Right.

389
00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:51.360
Right.

390
00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:52.300
So rude.

391
00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:52.540
You know what?

392
00:19:52.580 --> 00:20:04.560
This is really making me question why we named our show a deep cut that is so deep that even the creator does not recall.

393
00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:06.180
I like it.

394
00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:08.820
But I like the mystery for those that don't know.

395
00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:10.260
No, it's not.

396
00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:11.900
We are so thrilled to have you here.

397
00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:15.760
This is a big occasion in the world of the office.

398
00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:19.140
This is a huge anniversary that we've come here together to celebrate.

399
00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:23.100
It is the 20th anniversary of the office.

400
00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:23.700
Crazy.

401
00:20:24.140 --> 00:20:24.620
Crazy.

402
00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:25.560
Is it crazy, though?

403
00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:26.320
Oh, now, here's the thing.

404
00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:38.200
I realized that for a lot of times that people would say that I think the sort of expected thing is like, oh, I can't believe so much time has gone by.

405
00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:43.160
But then I was questioning, like, that didn't really sit right with me.

406
00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:46.880
And I was like, no, I think it feels like it's been a long time to me.

407
00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:48.020
So I don't know.

408
00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:49.140
How does it feel to you?

409
00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:51.640
I mean, I think it's a combo platter.

410
00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:55.820
I think sometimes it feels like a whole different existence.

411
00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:58.800
You know, it was such a chapter so long ago.

412
00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:09.140
And then sometimes we will hear a clip or revisit an episode and I'll remember, you know, what we were doing that week.

413
00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.420
And then you're right back there.

414
00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:17.280
So it's a little bit like going through an old family album of photos in a way.

415
00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:25.720
I had a weird experience because we are doing season 14 and 15 of King of the Hill.

416
00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:26.940
Oh, my gosh.

417
00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:28.160
If you can imagine that.

418
00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:28.760
Wow.

419
00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:30.200
And that's a much older show.

420
00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:30.680
Yeah.

421
00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:36.700
And so I was writing for those characters.

422
00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:43.920
And, you know, and I hadn't really thought about them since before our show started.

423
00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:49.960
And I was just so happy to kind of talk to the characters again.

424
00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:53.660
It was very kind of hippy-dippy thing going on in my head.

425
00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:55.360
But I was like, oh, my gosh, Peggy.

426
00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:56.260
Yes.

427
00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:57.640
I haven't talked to you in ages.

428
00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:58.520
That's right.

429
00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.300
Jenna said that one time.

430
00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:03.680
You said, you know, we have all stayed in touch.

431
00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:05.800
You know, the cast, we have this text thread.

432
00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:12.100
But you were like, I miss, like, Meredith and Kevin.

433
00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:13.340
And I miss Dwight.

434
00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:13.820
Yeah.

435
00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:14.220
Yeah.

436
00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:15.020
I see Rain.

437
00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:16.580
But I don't-

438
00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:17.060
But Rain's not Dwight.

439
00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:18.540
I'll never get to talk to Dwight again.

440
00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:19.040
Yeah.

441
00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:20.320
I'd love to talk to that guy.

442
00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:21.020
Yeah.

443
00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:22.320
Some weird dreaMs.

444
00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:26.180
You just got to eat, like, weird, cheesy things right before bed.

445
00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:29.560
You know, I do have dreams where we're shooting again.

446
00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:30.200
Yeah.

447
00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:33.120
And I'm back on set, and we're all there.

448
00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:38.180
And sometimes the dream is, like, we're making the show.

449
00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:42.680
But then sometimes the dream is that we've created a reunion show.

450
00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.160
It's like present day.

451
00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:46.380
So I've had both of these dreaMs.

452
00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.380
And is one of them a nightmare?

453
00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:52.540
Actually, the reunion one's a nightmare.

454
00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:55.820
So take that for what it's worth.

455
00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:56.880
It's usually me.

456
00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:01.640
It's usually wrapped up in that idea of, like, oh, my gosh.

457
00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:04.660
I didn't- I said yes to this, and I forgot to prepare.

458
00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:05.100
Yeah.

459
00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:08.280
And I don't remember how to be Pam, and I don't know what I'm doing.

460
00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:09.040
Yeah.

461
00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:09.260
Yeah.

462
00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:16.340
Well, I feel like it's incredibly stressful to have a show that you really care about on the air,

463
00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:23.000
because it can go south at the last second, and everybody can be mad, and, you know,

464
00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.560
you can ruin it.

465
00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:32.580
And it was such a nice feeling for me when we finally stopped producing new episodes of the show.

466
00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:34.080
So I was like, okay, it's safe.

467
00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:34.440
Did.

468
00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:34.940
Did that.

469
00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:36.040
Put it on the shelf.

470
00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:36.500
Put to bed.

471
00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:36.860
Yeah.

472
00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:38.140
Yeah.

473
00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:46.040
I still remember when I, you know, one of the things Jen and I did for this episode is we went and re-watched the pilot,

474
00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:51.720
and we went and watched the super fan cut of the pilot, because neither of us had seen that.

475
00:23:52.840 --> 00:24:01.580
And I still, like, when I watched it, what came back to me was that feeling when we would walk on the stage in the morning,

476
00:24:01.960 --> 00:24:03.720
like before we started filming.

477
00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:04.200
Mm-hmm.

478
00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:08.880
And we'd walk down that corridor, and we'd go in the back way, you know, past hair and makeup.

479
00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:09.640
Yeah.

480
00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:13.800
And then you were all of a sudden in the Dunder Mifflin offices.

481
00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:18.120
I just remembered all of that, how I would walk to my desk in the morning and...

482
00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:21.840
Are you picturing the offices from the pilot in season one?

483
00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:22.660
No, no.

484
00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:26.900
I'm picturing, I always picture the ones where we filmed the majority of the time.

485
00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:34.680
Although I do remember that office, because Oscar and I made such asses of ourselves, like,

486
00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:41.420
and when we filmed in Culver City, I think I've told this story before, that he and I decided to walk down the stairs,

487
00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:47.260
you know, because we filmed upstairs to our trailers, and we decided to walk swinging our arms left to right,

488
00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:50.660
left to right, and kicking our legs, and we made these noises.

489
00:24:50.880 --> 00:25:01.800
And we were being so silly, and behind us was Terry Weinberg and, like, all the network suits and people we didn't even know.

490
00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:05.200
We just knew they were important because they all went into the room to chat and stuff.

491
00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:07.520
And Oscar was like, oh, God, we're fired.

492
00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:08.380
We're fired.

493
00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:15.060
So I do remember that building very clearly, but I can remember what it felt like to walk on set.

494
00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:16.640
Do you remember what it felt like?

495
00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:17.400
Oh, yeah.

496
00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:19.320
That is something I wish I could do.

497
00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:22.880
I wish that – and to me, it's still there.

498
00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:25.480
I've never been back to those same stages.

499
00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:30.900
I know that there's, like, game shows and stuff that shoot there.

500
00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.020
I think Always Sunny in Philadelphia shot there.

501
00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:33.620
They have.

502
00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:34.140
Yeah.

503
00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:34.820
Yeah.

504
00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:35.520
Which is weird.

505
00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:39.420
Do you remember in season one – and this might have been the pilot,

506
00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:48.280
but I remember that to get to the stage, you had to go up a couple of stairs or down a couple of stairs,

507
00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:53.860
and there was a very low concrete ceiling lintel at the doorway.

508
00:25:53.860 --> 00:26:02.680
And I would come running onto the set to give a note and bash my head against this lintel.

509
00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:13.160
And then eventually they put a big rubber bumper there that said, like, Greg Daniels Memorial Head Bumper or something so that I wouldn't knock myself out.

510
00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:14.780
That is so funny.

511
00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:15.660
Trying to rush in.

512
00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:15.900
Yeah.

513
00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:16.620
Oh, my gosh.

514
00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:20.620
I mean, I never had that problem because I never –

515
00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:22.240
No, that's not a problem you have to worry about.

516
00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:23.820
I clear a doorway very easily.

517
00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:28.020
When's the last time you watched an episode of The Office start to finish?

518
00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:31.160
Oh, I watch them periodically.

519
00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:39.300
Like, you know, it comes up because, as you probably know, I'm doing the next documentary from the same documentary crew.

520
00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:39.840
Yes.

521
00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:40.800
This new show.

522
00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:41.900
And –

523
00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:42.980
The paper.

524
00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:44.000
Yes, the paper.

525
00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:54.160
And so a lot of times we'll have some things that we wanted to do differently and then they may not be 100% working out.

526
00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:58.640
And I'll go back and I'll check the original show and I'll be like, oh, you know,

527
00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:07.600
that's why we had the blinds that are two inches apart and not one inch apart so you can see people's faces and things like that.

528
00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:08.580
That's my shots.

529
00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:09.000
Yeah.

530
00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:09.580
Yeah.

531
00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:11.040
I love going back and watching the show.

532
00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:17.240
And then, of course, you know, I have a 15-year-old and she doesn't know what it is and she's watching it.

533
00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:18.860
For the first time?

534
00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:19.120
Yeah.

535
00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:20.620
So sometimes I'll watch it with her.

536
00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:21.540
Yeah.

537
00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:24.320
She's just plowing through all that era though.

538
00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:25.480
She just finished New Girl.

539
00:27:25.780 --> 00:27:26.180
Uh-huh.

540
00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:27.860
She just started Arrested Development.

541
00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:28.980
She's just like –

542
00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:29.300
Yeah.

543
00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:32.340
That's a pretty great time in television comedy actually.

544
00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:45.400
Yeah, we did that – part of our pandemic watch with our kids was Friends and then The Office and, you know, Isabel just turned to me one day and said, Mom,

545
00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:51.380
you're so mean to everybody when we were watching The Office.

546
00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:53.080
I was like, I know, but it's not me.

547
00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:56.000
But, yeah, that was kind of hard for her to reconcile.

548
00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:56.660
Yeah.

549
00:27:56.660 --> 00:27:58.220
My kids still haven't watched The Office.

550
00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:00.260
We're watching Modern Family right now.

551
00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:01.620
Yes, we did watch that too.

552
00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:02.740
And it's so good.

553
00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:03.060
Yeah.

554
00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:04.020
That's a good one.

555
00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:12.900
And I wondered, like, would – okay, now that you kind of have this, like, there's their version of talking heads and I know they speak to the camera.

556
00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:15.560
I'm like, maybe now they'll want to watch The Office.

557
00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:17.800
But they haven't expressed interest yet.

558
00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:18.060
Yeah.

559
00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:19.020
They'll get there.

560
00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:27.520
I found that when you remove the jealousy of them winning the Emmy every year –

561
00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:29.260
This show is really great.

562
00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:31.540
It really improved my experience watching Modern Family.

563
00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:32.300
Yeah.

564
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:33.180
Recently.

565
00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:34.380
I know.

566
00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:36.200
We did go up against them a lot.

567
00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:37.540
We really did.

568
00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:38.320
All right.

569
00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:45.540
Well, Greg, one of the questions we always ask our guests, how did it come about that you got your job creating The Office?

570
00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:47.300
Yes.

571
00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:47.660
Okay.

572
00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:56.440
So, basically what happened was I had been doing King of the Hill and it was very, you know, good experience for me.

573
00:28:56.600 --> 00:28:59.720
And it was probably around season eight.

574
00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:02.900
And I was not really needed there.

575
00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:06.620
And my deal at 20th was up.

576
00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:08.320
And so, I was a free agent.

577
00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:12.240
And I was looking around at all sorts of different things to be the next project.

578
00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:20.340
And I had some real interesting meetings about The Muppet Show, which someone else did later, and a couple of other things.

579
00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:32.020
And my agent sent me a tape, a VHS tape called The Office before Christmas break, like 20 – probably 2003, December maybe.

580
00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.380
And it was a little bit of a boring title, and it was a little bit of a boring title, and I didn't watch it.

581
00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:42.620
And then so, he called me like January 3rd and said, all right, I'm going to send it to the next guy if you don't want to do it.

582
00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:44.720
And I was like, hang on, hang on, let me watch it, and I'll watch it tonight.

583
00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:50.300
And I started it, and I just fell in love with it, the first season of six episodes.

584
00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:52.420
And I stayed up late watching all of them.

585
00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:55.600
And then I was like, this stuff is absolutely brilliant.

586
00:29:55.600 --> 00:30:07.880
And it doesn't feel like American TV, but I desperately want to meet the people who made it and just try and figure out how it works because it's the best thing that I had seen.

587
00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:13.360
So, I ended up having a meeting with Ricky and Steven when they came to the U.S.

588
00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:15.620
to interview people to adapt it.

589
00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:24.240
And it turned out that I had written Ricky Gervais' favorite Simpsons episode, which was called Homer Badman.

590
00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:29.960
And so, that was good, kind of getting us off on the right foot.

591
00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:43.320
And a lot of the stuff that I brought to King of the Hill had resonance with them in terms of the realism of the show and the sincerity, I guess, at times.

592
00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:46.640
And so, we kind of hit it off.

593
00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:49.720
And so, I said, okay, maybe I'll adapt this.

594
00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:56.220
And at the time, we definitely thought it would be probably on HBO or something like that.

595
00:30:56.900 --> 00:30:59.780
And Kevin Reilly was interested.

596
00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:00.920
And he was at FX.

597
00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:08.440
And, you know, he very much got the show and was enthusiastic about it.

598
00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:10.840
So, we were like, all right, well, FX would work.

599
00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:11.580
That's pretty cool.

600
00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:12.180
Has an edge.

601
00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:12.580
Yeah.

602
00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:13.380
Kind of edgy.

603
00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:14.320
It'll do something different.

604
00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:17.200
And so, I signed on to adapt it.

605
00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:22.180
And then Kevin went to NBC and he still wanted it.

606
00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:25.520
And then Silverman was like, yeah, yeah, let's do it for NBC.

607
00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:30.800
And I was like, you know, it doesn't feel like NBC, you know.

608
00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:32.460
NBC was multicam.

609
00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:41.740
And so, going into it, I was kind of like, all right, well, if we do it for NBC, we'll probably won't win, probably won't be successful.

610
00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:53.040
But maybe we'll like nudge the ship of comedy in a direction that I would rather see it float in than the complete multicam direction.

611
00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:57.760
And, yeah, so that's how I got into it.

612
00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:10.800
And then the other thing was I did not think that the original show was ever going to air in America because when you think of other British adaptations like All in the Family,

613
00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:14.920
like no one's ever seen the British show that All in the Family was based on.

614
00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:27.040
And so, I just figured it was obvious that they wouldn't run the British original about two months before we aired, which they did.

615
00:32:28.160 --> 00:32:29.140
That was scary.

616
00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:37.840
Well, I'm curious, looking back from your perspective, when did you know that our version was going to stick?

617
00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:42.340
Or when did you feel like we were sort of a hit show, I guess?

618
00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.340
Well, that all happened season two.

619
00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:51.980
I don't know if you remember the pilot, but I had a little some words after the pilot and we all went out to a bar, I think, when it stopped shooting.

620
00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:53.200
Do you remember that?

621
00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:54.400
Oh, and we said goodbye.

622
00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:54.800
Yeah.

623
00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:57.140
We said, I'll see you on the next job.

624
00:32:57.140 --> 00:32:59.520
I actually found pictures from that.

625
00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:00.020
Yeah.

626
00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:07.360
And I was telling Jenna this because when we did our book, we went through and looked at a ton of pictures and we found some from the pilot and things like that.

627
00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:12.460
But I actually found pictures from that bar that we all went to.

628
00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:12.840
Yeah.

629
00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:16.840
And I remember thinking, well, that was such a fun experience.

630
00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:18.260
It was completely worth it.

631
00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:25.300
It didn't need – it doesn't need to be successful for me to have found happiness in shooting that pilot and meeting everybody.

632
00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:26.680
And it was super good.

633
00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.980
And I remember also saying something similar at the end of season one.

634
00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:32.880
I was like, all right, this will probably tank.

635
00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:39.180
You know, our six episodes of season one, our very short season one.

636
00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:45.920
But, you know, it was very exciting in season two because it just kept going up and up and up in season two.

637
00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:53.560
And by the end of season two, I think, like on Casino Night, we were kind of a successful show by then, I think.

638
00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:56.220
What do you attribute that to in season two?

639
00:33:56.460 --> 00:33:58.240
Do you think it's changes we made?

640
00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:00.460
Was it just people getting used to it?

641
00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:01.580
Like what do you think it was?

642
00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:09.600
Well, from my perspective, Kevin Riley wasn't sure he was going to pick up season two.

643
00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:20.580
And there was a lot of drama before the upfronts for that year where you – I don't know if this is – I think this is known in some places.

644
00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:26.760
But the company kind of lowered the budget and stripped everybody's bonuses out.

645
00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:31.880
And, you know, they really did a lot of belt tightening to bring us back.

646
00:34:31.880 --> 00:34:40.260
And I had to go pitch a new direction to Kevin before he would greenlight the next season.

647
00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:46.940
And this actually does go back a little bit to King of the Hill again because what I had done at King of the Hill was say,

648
00:34:46.940 --> 00:35:02.560
OK, Hank has a lot of unlikable aspects to him, but I'm going to build all these likable features to him to like undo whatever anybody's misgivings might be.

649
00:35:02.780 --> 00:35:11.220
Like if he maybe appears a little misogynistic at times, then I'm going to make him super supportive of his wife and also, you know,

650
00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:16.060
a complete Boy Scout prude when it comes to his niece that's living with him.

651
00:35:16.060 --> 00:35:17.900
And, you know, various things like that.

652
00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:21.220
So going into it, I was like, all right, I was very,

653
00:35:21.320 --> 00:35:31.380
very so much in love with the original British show that we didn't make too many changes to Michael's character in the first season.

654
00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:35.340
So I was kind of like, I think I know what I need to do.

655
00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:37.080
I need to do the same thing I did with Hank.

656
00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:45.360
And I had a list of different things that would rehabilitate Michael's character in the eyes of the audience.

657
00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:50.340
And they became maybe the first six or eight episodes.

658
00:35:50.660 --> 00:35:59.200
So the emotional plot of the Dundies is, you know, the staff is very irritated by Michael.

659
00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:03.380
But if somebody outside the staff criticizes him, they'll defend him.

660
00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:11.420
And so that's sort of like a family dynamic kind of thing where it solves the problem of is he really hated by his staff?

661
00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:12.560
No, he's not.

662
00:36:12.740 --> 00:36:17.700
He's, you know, they have some sort of family feeling towards him.

663
00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:23.280
And then there was Booze Cruise was from my initial napkin that I went in to pitch.

664
00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:30.600
Kevin Riley was let Michael give some really good advice to somebody that the audience really wants him to say.

665
00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:37.760
So when Michael tells Jim, never give up, that's exactly what the audience wanted somebody to tell Jim.

666
00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:43.420
And, you know, so the idea that Michael has that insight is good for Michael, you know.

667
00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:47.920
And in the coup, he's actually pretty good at sales.

668
00:36:47.920 --> 00:36:49.800
So he's not a total incompetent.

669
00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:54.660
And the idea was like there's something called the Peter Principle.

670
00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:55.180
Do you know that?

671
00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:55.580
Yes.

672
00:36:55.580 --> 00:36:55.880
Yes.

673
00:36:56.160 --> 00:36:56.320
Yes.

674
00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:56.660
Yeah.

675
00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:59.600
So the idea was he's actually really good as a sales manager.

676
00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:02.700
He just shouldn't have been turned into –

677
00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:03.840
Promoted into the general manager.

678
00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:04.140
That's right.

679
00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:07.020
Or the branch manager.

680
00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:09.540
So, you know, the different things like that.

681
00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:18.380
I had maybe six or eight things that I thought would fix his character over the course of the season.

682
00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:32.520
So from my perspective, a lot of it is these things were chipping away at whatever the problems were and kind of making a new version of Michael where he was more of a, you know,

683
00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:37.760
exasperating uncle than like an evil boss or something.

684
00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:38.060
A jerky boss.

685
00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:38.300
Yeah.

686
00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:44.940
I love hearing about that process of you laying out the emotional plot for a character.

687
00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:47.440
That's the stuff I always find so fascinating.

688
00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:50.200
Did you do that with multiple characters on the show?

689
00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:54.440
Did you know what their emotional plot was or were you charting that?

690
00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:55.280
Oh, yeah.

691
00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:58.960
That's a huge part of the writer's room.

692
00:37:59.240 --> 00:37:59.440
Yeah.

693
00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:05.220
Yeah, and one of the big things with Pam, for instance, is, okay, what's going on with Pam?

694
00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:09.780
What are the obstacles to Pam and Jim getting together, right?

695
00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:25.680
So the show starts and the obstacle is Roy and she's engaged and then, you know, he takes his shot and she's not ready to break up with Roy or to, you know, cheat or anything.

696
00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:36.680
So she turns him down and then Jim goes to Connecticut and by the time he comes back, he has a new girlfriend, which from his perspective is okay because he was turned down.

697
00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:50.560
And, you know, Pam is watching him with Karen and, you know, the big obstacle at the second half of that season was sort of Pam's confidence level.

698
00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:55.260
And does she get the courage to say what she wants?

699
00:38:55.460 --> 00:39:07.300
And in beach games, when she walks across the coals, that's, you know, that's a moment that was like a year in preparation because it's getting her to feel enough.

700
00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:08.340
Emboldened.

701
00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:08.800
Yes.

702
00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:09.160
Yeah.

703
00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:09.740
Yeah.

704
00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:10.640
Yeah.

705
00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:11.780
Love that.

706
00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:12.920
So good.

707
00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:13.920
There's a lot of that kind of stuff.

708
00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:14.160
Yeah.

709
00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:24.820
And then my writing predilection is to have an emotional story that is extremely well worked out,

710
00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:38.800
but is what I would call like underwater and only surfaces at one moment in the show to do its job and then disappears underwater again.

711
00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:50.260
But you have to get that submarine at the right place, you know, you have to plot its path all the way when it's not noticed by the audience, you know,

712
00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:51.800
all the way through the episode.

713
00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:52.520
Yeah.

714
00:39:52.820 --> 00:40:00.440
And so one of the problems with, for me, doing rewrites is sometimes you forget because you're just looking at the surface of the plot.

715
00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:05.260
But you're forgetting that some of these lines are also charting, of course, for the submarine.

716
00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:08.500
And, you know, you're like, I got a better joke.

717
00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:15.040
And you switch the joke to something else that works for the surface plot, but doesn't help the submarine get where it needs to go.

718
00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:23.620
And so you're constantly balancing the sort of surface story with like the heart story that's lying underneath a little bit.

719
00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:24.040
Yeah.

720
00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:32.160
Before King of the Hill, I had the experience of working on other shows where they didn't take the emotional storyline very seriously.

721
00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:37.980
And a lot of the comedy writers at that time had a term which they used called treacle cutter.

722
00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:46.340
And the idea was that at the end of an episode, there'd be some treacle that was some little piece of like awe hug.

723
00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:55.900
And then that they needed to have a joke after that called the treacle cutter because they were so disgusted that they had just, you know, done some sort of treacle.

724
00:40:56.140 --> 00:40:59.060
And I was like, well, you don't have to be embarrassed if it isn't treacle.

725
00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:05.600
You know, if it actually works and you actually feel something, then you don't need a little treacle cutter, you know.

726
00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:10.460
But it's a lot of underappreciated effort to put all those little beats in.

727
00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:14.780
There's so many good Jim and Pam ones, too, when you rewatch it.

728
00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:20.120
I mean, one of my favorites is when they're just listening to the voicemails at the end of the day.

729
00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:20.520
Yeah.

730
00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:22.160
That was one of my favorites.

731
00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:23.200
Yeah.

732
00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:32.020
So, like, I think business school is possibly my favorite episode, but I think that one is such a great Pam Michael moment.

733
00:41:32.780 --> 00:41:32.940
Yeah.

734
00:41:33.420 --> 00:41:34.340
At the art show.

735
00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:35.280
Yeah, at the art show.

736
00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:35.720
Yeah.

737
00:41:35.840 --> 00:41:39.400
When he's the only one who is really enthusiastic about her art.

738
00:41:39.760 --> 00:41:40.100
Yeah.

739
00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:40.500
Yeah.

740
00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:41.860
He's the one that showed up.

741
00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:45.900
He's also the only person that knew Sprinkles, like, who got upset about Sprinkles.

742
00:41:46.060 --> 00:41:46.920
I just watched that.

743
00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:47.200
Yeah.

744
00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:47.680
Yeah.

745
00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:48.640
In Fun Run.

746
00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:49.220
Yeah.

747
00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:49.700
Yeah.

748
00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:51.380
He did get the name wrong.

749
00:41:51.620 --> 00:41:52.260
He got it right.

750
00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:53.040
He got it right.

751
00:41:53.200 --> 00:41:53.940
The first time.

752
00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:54.300
No, he did.

753
00:41:54.500 --> 00:41:56.100
And then he was back to Michael.

754
00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:58.540
But he literally turned and goes, oh, no.

755
00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:01.140
He's like, he was heartbroken for her.

756
00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:01.440
Yeah.

757
00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:09.660
Is there any storyline or card that was on the wall that you didn't get to do that you still wish?

758
00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:11.640
We have that whole Pet Day script.

759
00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:13.500
We have a whole script that didn't get printed.

760
00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:13.860
Yes.

761
00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.560
Can you share a little bit about what that script's story was all about?

762
00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:18.040
Yeah.

763
00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:21.340
So, we had, I think, it would have been season one, right?

764
00:42:21.340 --> 00:42:29.060
So, I think we had only enough budget for five episodes, but we had enough script budget for six.

765
00:42:29.060 --> 00:42:37.060
So, I wrote an extra episode called Pet Day where Michael encourages everybody to bring their pets in.

766
00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:40.540
And I think Dwight brought in a Demi Wolf.

767
00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:51.340
Anyway, at the end of the day, I was like, I'm going to make sure all the writers get a script and I'll take that one and shelve it.

768
00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:53.300
And maybe it'll come back later.

769
00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:54.420
And it never came back later.

770
00:42:54.420 --> 00:43:01.960
I have to imagine if you were Kent Sabornak and you got handed Pet Day, he was just like, how am I?

771
00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:03.700
Because I do remember the script.

772
00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:05.340
How do I get this Demi Wolf?

773
00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:09.300
But then also there were like, there was a bird that was in it.

774
00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:12.240
And this was going to be a lot of animals on set.

775
00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:12.700
Yeah.

776
00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:17.080
I think a lot of people would probably have said unproducible.

777
00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:20.000
I hope Angela had 13 of her cats there.

778
00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:21.780
I got to go back and look at that script.

779
00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:23.400
I know she had cats.

780
00:43:23.980 --> 00:43:29.200
You know who knew almost every single of Angela Martin's cats was Billie Eilish.

781
00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:31.260
Yes, she's a huge fan.

782
00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:32.240
She came on the show.

783
00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:34.180
We played an office trivia game with her.

784
00:43:34.280 --> 00:43:36.740
And I mean, I was struggling to name them all.

785
00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:37.540
And she got them.

786
00:43:37.960 --> 00:43:40.260
I was like, wow, that's amazing.

787
00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:47.660
Are there any celebrities that you've met that have just gone on and on and on about the office and maybe surprised you?

788
00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:51.580
Well, I love that your premise is that I'm meeting a lot of celebrities.

789
00:43:53.060 --> 00:43:55.560
You're all the fancy person in our mind.

790
00:43:55.700 --> 00:43:57.200
You're out there hop-nubbing.

791
00:43:57.900 --> 00:43:58.220
Yeah.

792
00:43:58.400 --> 00:43:59.080
No, no.

793
00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:02.880
I mean, I heard somewhere that Spielberg really liked the show.

794
00:44:03.220 --> 00:44:03.800
Like, he would be a big sovereignty to me.

795
00:44:03.800 --> 00:44:04.400
No way.

796
00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:05.920
This is news to us.

797
00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:07.060
Oh, my gosh.

798
00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:09.120
So, that's good.

799
00:44:09.280 --> 00:44:10.360
Jenna has a good one.

800
00:44:10.700 --> 00:44:11.440
Tell the story.

801
00:44:11.440 --> 00:44:13.120
About Angelina Jolie?

802
00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:14.060
Yes, tell it.

803
00:44:14.500 --> 00:44:21.340
Yeah, I was at a party and Angelina Jolie was there with a few of her kids.

804
00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:24.000
And I clocked her.

805
00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:28.960
And I noticed, like, out of my peripheral vision that she was walking right toward me.

806
00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:31.100
And I was like, who's behind me?

807
00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:32.340
Who's she walking to?

808
00:44:32.340 --> 00:44:35.580
And she came right up to me and introduced herself.

809
00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:37.860
She said, hi, my name's Angie.

810
00:44:38.240 --> 00:44:41.760
And I just wanted to tell you, my family, we are such a huge fan of the office.

811
00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:42.780
And you're like, no autographs.

812
00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:43.260
I know.

813
00:44:43.340 --> 00:44:44.600
I was like, get out of here.

814
00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:47.660
She was so nice and warm.

815
00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:50.100
And I was like, what?

816
00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:55.160
And she said, I'm here with my kids and we watch it together and they're huge fans.

817
00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:57.000
Would you mind saying hello to them?

818
00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:58.100
And I said, yes.

819
00:44:58.260 --> 00:45:01.160
And I walked over and talked to them all.

820
00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:02.820
And she was so nice.

821
00:45:02.960 --> 00:45:04.080
She was so nice.

822
00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:08.180
She was like, oh, we should get lunch sometime or we should.

823
00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:10.300
And I was like, yes, of course we should.

824
00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:14.180
But then I'm like, I don't know how to follow up on that.

825
00:45:14.360 --> 00:45:18.320
Yeah, is that like a real let's have lunch or is that kind of like party chit chat?

826
00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:19.660
She said it twice.

827
00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:22.220
Okay, then you should have gotten her information.

828
00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:31.080
But also one time Michael Douglas offered me his house in Spain to vacation at because

829
00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:32.140
I did a movie with him.

830
00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:35.220
He said it three times on two different days.

831
00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:38.460
So like the first day he said it twice.

832
00:45:38.540 --> 00:45:42.100
He's like, oh, Catherine and I have a house in Spain.

833
00:45:42.100 --> 00:45:42.960
You can use it.

834
00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:50.240
And the next day when Lee was visiting, he said, did Jenna tell you about our place in Spain?

835
00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:52.420
You two are welcome to use it.

836
00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:52.840
Wow.

837
00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:57.720
And yet I have not vacationed at his house in Spain.

838
00:45:57.920 --> 00:45:59.200
I just I don't know how to.

839
00:45:59.380 --> 00:46:01.000
How do you transition that?

840
00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:01.220
Yeah.

841
00:46:01.320 --> 00:46:03.400
How do I make that happen?

842
00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:05.160
I would have followed up on that.

843
00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:10.740
Also, maybe if you just have houses in Spain and whatnot, you're very gracious about it.

844
00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:14.720
Maybe you're just like, go to my house in Monaco.

845
00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:15.900
I don't know.

846
00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:17.340
Maybe there's a tax reason.

847
00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:20.060
It's like, I can write it off.

848
00:46:20.060 --> 00:46:20.800
You're so cynical.

849
00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:23.120
You're like, what's in it for Michael?

850
00:46:23.460 --> 00:46:24.680
I can write it off.

851
00:46:24.920 --> 00:46:25.380
Okay.

852
00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:28.800
I have a question since we're reflecting on 20 years.

853
00:46:29.320 --> 00:46:34.100
This is, we were debating about including this question and we got tickled about it.

854
00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:36.100
So just take it with a grain of salt.

855
00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:43.780
Is there a storyline or episode that you think we maybe jumped the shark with when you look back?

856
00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:45.600
Oh my goodness.

857
00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:45.740
Yes.

858
00:46:45.940 --> 00:46:49.800
We almost jumped a horse over Niagara Falls.

859
00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:50.140
Yes.

860
00:46:50.360 --> 00:46:50.720
But we didn't.

861
00:46:50.740 --> 00:46:51.340
That we know.

862
00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:52.640
That could have been the moment.

863
00:46:53.120 --> 00:46:53.260
Yeah.

864
00:46:53.800 --> 00:46:54.180
Boy.

865
00:46:54.560 --> 00:47:05.100
Well, I mean, the notion of jump the shark for me is like there's a dividing line between the show was good and then the show was kind of cheesy.

866
00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:05.200
Right.

867
00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:09.300
And I think that's not my intention with the question.

868
00:47:09.300 --> 00:47:18.620
I think it's more like, was there a moment or a decision where you're like, yeah, if I had it to do all over again, I wouldn't have gone down that road.

869
00:47:19.620 --> 00:47:20.700
I'm pretty proud of the show.

870
00:47:20.900 --> 00:47:22.520
I'm not going to go there.

871
00:47:22.520 --> 00:47:26.580
And also I think that there's – I learned on King of the Hill,

872
00:47:26.660 --> 00:47:34.340
I was very worried about – we had an episode where – and I know I've been talking a lot about King of the Hill on this Office Ladies podcaSt.

873
00:47:34.340 --> 00:47:44.920
But we had an episode where they – all of Hank and his friends became volunteer firemen and they were just like the Three Stooges go to a firehouse.

874
00:47:45.140 --> 00:47:51.340
And I was completely sweating it because there was no subterranean emotional story on that one.

875
00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:53.440
It was just all goofball stuff.

876
00:47:53.440 --> 00:47:56.140
And I was like, well, that was it.

877
00:47:56.220 --> 00:47:56.960
We broke the show.

878
00:47:57.240 --> 00:48:01.900
And then a whole bunch of people were like, that's my favorite episode.

879
00:48:01.940 --> 00:48:03.660
And I was like, oh, okay.

880
00:48:03.680 --> 00:48:07.720
It's just a different – it's just slightly different gear.

881
00:48:07.940 --> 00:48:08.080
Yeah.

882
00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:11.200
And once you have established what your main gear is,

883
00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:19.200
it's kind of fun to do something a little broader or a little weirder or something because there's going to be some people who – that's the standout.

884
00:48:19.640 --> 00:48:23.720
Well, you're sitting across from two people who love the show and think it's perfect.

885
00:48:24.100 --> 00:48:24.340
So –

886
00:48:24.340 --> 00:48:25.580
What would your – yeah, let's ask.

887
00:48:25.700 --> 00:48:27.600
You've been doing the in-depth rewatch.

888
00:48:27.720 --> 00:48:28.880
Where did we go wrong?

889
00:48:29.040 --> 00:48:31.760
There's one episode that I would remove.

890
00:48:32.480 --> 00:48:34.220
I feel bad saying it.

891
00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:37.160
It's our clip show that we were forced to do.

892
00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:37.600
Oh, oh.

893
00:48:37.640 --> 00:48:38.560
Remember that episode?

894
00:48:38.720 --> 00:48:41.300
It's like I don't even know if you call it an episode.

895
00:48:41.300 --> 00:48:42.760
It was called The Banker.

896
00:48:43.140 --> 00:48:43.360
Yeah.

897
00:48:43.620 --> 00:48:44.880
I wouldn't – it's – I felt like –

898
00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:46.240
It was like a wraparound.

899
00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:48.280
It was like a couple minutes of a wraparound.

900
00:48:48.280 --> 00:48:49.640
That's what he made us do is what I'm feeling.

901
00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:49.690
Yes.

902
00:48:49.690 --> 00:48:51.320
I don't remember the backstory on that.

903
00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:59.620
It doesn't – well, and the thing is is that the part that was original where this banker comes in to –

904
00:48:59.620 --> 00:49:02.120
Oh, and he's fantastic, the guy that played the banker.

905
00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:02.920
Yeah, he's a great actor.

906
00:49:03.060 --> 00:49:07.620
And he has some scenes with Toby that then justify all of the flashing back.

907
00:49:07.860 --> 00:49:09.440
All that stuff is great.

908
00:49:09.440 --> 00:49:14.740
It was an interesting premise for an episode, which is that this insurance bank, right?

909
00:49:14.880 --> 00:49:17.980
He had to come in and he's like, do you have any HR problems?

910
00:49:17.980 --> 00:49:24.720
Like this idea that Toby, who knows everything wrong with the office, is having to kind of protect the office.

911
00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:28.680
I would have loved for the whole episode to be that.

912
00:49:28.860 --> 00:49:31.020
But instead, we had to keep going to all these clips.

913
00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:38.280
So I think it doesn't – that is the one that – it's hard to even say it was an episode of The Office.

914
00:49:38.480 --> 00:49:40.900
Yeah, it almost doesn't register for me.

915
00:49:40.900 --> 00:49:42.200
I don't even remember it, really.

916
00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:42.620
Right.

917
00:49:42.980 --> 00:49:43.140
Yeah.

918
00:49:43.360 --> 00:49:48.820
But I think otherwise, in watching all the episodes, I don't think we ever jumped the shark.

919
00:49:48.820 --> 00:49:53.320
I don't think that there's – I think we did just have different gears.

920
00:49:53.840 --> 00:50:00.420
And then in re-watching the show, when we get to one of those episodes with a different gear, I am excited about it.

921
00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:00.720
Yeah.

922
00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:06.400
And again, people will come up and they'll love that kind of stuff.

923
00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:18.180
And the other thing about the show is because of the cold opens and because of the subplots, for me, there's memorable moments that I have no idea what episode they're in.

924
00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:22.440
You know, like parkour, cold open.

925
00:50:22.700 --> 00:50:26.740
Like, I don't know where – what episode that's in, what season that's in.

926
00:50:26.880 --> 00:50:27.100
Yeah.

927
00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:30.080
It's just such a great, you know, sketch comedy.

928
00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:31.460
We had such great cold opens.

929
00:50:31.660 --> 00:50:34.460
And, you know, we're watching the webisodes now as well.

930
00:50:34.640 --> 00:50:36.580
And the webisodes are great.

931
00:50:36.820 --> 00:50:43.780
I just love learning new things about characters that ring true, but maybe you just haven't seen them as much.

932
00:50:43.780 --> 00:50:54.500
Even – there was this very funny one where Gabe is so incredibly jealous that Oscar has a blog on the Saber page that Joe Bennett loves.

933
00:50:54.980 --> 00:50:58.800
And Gabe just covets Joe Bennett's favor.

934
00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:00.880
So he tries to start a podcaSt.

935
00:51:01.380 --> 00:51:03.680
And it's just – it just cracked me up.

936
00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:05.220
You know what's so crazy?

937
00:51:05.480 --> 00:51:09.480
I can't – I forgot her name was Joe Bennett.

938
00:51:09.480 --> 00:51:13.600
And I'm working with Joe Bennett, the animator now.

939
00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:19.560
He does a show called Common Side Effects with Office writer Steve Healy.

940
00:51:19.820 --> 00:51:23.940
The two of them created it for the animation company that I do with Mike Judge.

941
00:51:24.560 --> 00:51:28.960
And so I've been working with and talking to Joe Bennett for such a long time.

942
00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:30.860
I forgot.

943
00:51:31.220 --> 00:51:32.240
I forgot that was her name.

944
00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:35.540
You did have a funny expression come on your face when I was mentioning it.

945
00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:36.900
All right.

946
00:51:37.000 --> 00:51:38.360
I have another question for you.

947
00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:42.820
If you were a character on the show, Greg, which character –

948
00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:43.480
I am a character on the show.

949
00:51:43.560 --> 00:51:44.280
I'm Michael's neighbor.

950
00:51:44.440 --> 00:51:44.840
I know.

951
00:51:44.840 --> 00:51:45.700
You're the yarn salesman.

952
00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:46.860
You're the yarn salesman.

953
00:51:46.860 --> 00:51:47.740
We loved it.

954
00:51:48.100 --> 00:51:57.480
So if you weren't the yarn salesman that lives next to Michael in the condo, who would you identify most with and what main cast character would you be?

955
00:51:57.480 --> 00:52:09.520
Oh, well, look, you know, I'm not going to pretend that I have the necessary charisma to be the star of the show.

956
00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:21.340
But as somebody who in the social world of the office was the boss and was previously the boss on the prior show that I did,

957
00:52:21.560 --> 00:52:29.080
I tend to identify with boss problems and the way people interact with Michael.

958
00:52:29.080 --> 00:52:38.040
So, for instance, the episode where Michael has to fire someone on Halloween but still wants to be friends with them, you know what I mean?

959
00:52:38.220 --> 00:52:41.940
Like that's a real boss point of view kind of a storyline.

960
00:52:42.380 --> 00:52:46.660
And, you know, just sort of – also, Steve and I are pretty much the same age.

961
00:52:46.660 --> 00:52:56.000
And so, like, the idea that, you know, he's super into – I want to pump you up, you know.

962
00:52:57.720 --> 00:53:03.340
All these things that are, like, you know, hit exactly right for somebody of that age group.

963
00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:05.860
So I would probably identify with Michael.

964
00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:20.340
I'm curious.

965
00:53:20.340 --> 00:53:23.540
We never shot an episode in Scranton.

966
00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:25.660
And I think it was a cost thing.

967
00:53:25.900 --> 00:53:26.180
Yeah.

968
00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:26.880
We tried.

969
00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:27.580
Yeah.

970
00:53:27.580 --> 00:53:31.400
We definitely had all these great plans to go shoot there.

971
00:53:31.580 --> 00:53:40.160
The weird thing about Scranton is it's remarkably beautiful in a way that Panorama City has trouble, you know,

972
00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:44.520
measuring up to it because there's beautiful trees all around Scranton.

973
00:53:44.540 --> 00:53:45.320
And there's that lake.

974
00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:46.460
It's really pretty.

975
00:53:46.660 --> 00:53:46.820
Yeah.

976
00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:50.640
And, you know, and it was a big deal in the 1800s.

977
00:53:50.740 --> 00:53:54.460
So the buildings in the downtown are, like, these cool stone buildings.

978
00:53:54.460 --> 00:54:00.240
And it's just got, like, a lot of beauty and character that we shortchanged it.

979
00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:03.760
But I did try to shoot there.

980
00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:11.800
And part of it is the difficulty of starting the season with Scranton or something like that.

981
00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:16.740
It was like you couldn't put it in the middle of the season because it would just blow the whole schedule up.

982
00:54:16.740 --> 00:54:19.900
And so it either had to be at the very beginning or the very end.

983
00:54:20.460 --> 00:54:21.240
And I don't know.

984
00:54:21.400 --> 00:54:22.680
It just escaped us.

985
00:54:23.060 --> 00:54:26.920
Parks and Rec went to England to shoot one season.

986
00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:32.260
And the only way to do that was to put it like the first three days of shooting for the season were in England.

987
00:54:32.680 --> 00:54:35.840
And then, you know, it would get, like, salted in later.

988
00:54:36.220 --> 00:54:40.060
Well, like I said, I've been watching Modern Family with my family.

989
00:54:40.180 --> 00:54:40.880
Did they go to Hawaii?

990
00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:42.280
They have gone to Hawaii.

991
00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:42.740
Yeah.

992
00:54:42.860 --> 00:54:45.760
They have, I believe, they've gone to Australia.

993
00:54:46.540 --> 00:54:48.120
They're going a lot of places.

994
00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:48.860
Yeah.

995
00:54:48.860 --> 00:54:50.440
Hey, we went to Long Beach.

996
00:54:51.220 --> 00:54:51.960
And –

997
00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:53.360
Well, you got to go to Niagara.

998
00:54:53.360 --> 00:54:54.460
I got to go to Niagara.

999
00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:56.260
And that was actually really cool.

1000
00:54:56.260 --> 00:55:01.280
I mean, that was – I don't know if I would have gone to Niagara Falls otherwise in my life.

1001
00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:03.120
And it's one of the wonders of the world.

1002
00:55:03.340 --> 00:55:04.280
So that was pretty cool.

1003
00:55:04.780 --> 00:55:08.240
And Steve went to New York, like, with the skeleton crew.

1004
00:55:08.360 --> 00:55:08.640
Yes.

1005
00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:08.920
We had that.

1006
00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:09.700
That was super fun.

1007
00:55:09.700 --> 00:55:11.040
I was in the van for that.

1008
00:55:11.260 --> 00:55:15.740
That was a crazy thing because our permits, you know,

1009
00:55:15.800 --> 00:55:22.820
we didn't – we weren't like a movie where we would shut down the street and get all these extras to come in and pretend to be real New Yorkers.

1010
00:55:22.820 --> 00:55:28.560
We were in two vans and basically a van would roll up to Times Square.

1011
00:55:29.120 --> 00:55:30.220
Steve would jump out.

1012
00:55:30.900 --> 00:55:32.380
Randall would jump out with the camera.

1013
00:55:33.060 --> 00:55:35.680
And I think I directed that episode.

1014
00:55:35.880 --> 00:55:37.560
And so I was out there.

1015
00:55:37.720 --> 00:55:39.040
Just sort of guerrilla style.

1016
00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:42.460
And it would be guerrilla style until somebody went, it's a 40-year-old virgin.

1017
00:55:42.660 --> 00:55:49.020
And then suddenly – because it was right after the movie came out, crowds would start to jam in on us.

1018
00:55:49.120 --> 00:55:50.540
And we'd be like, well, we got what we got.

1019
00:55:50.540 --> 00:55:52.900
And we'd jump back in the van and we'd go to someplace else.

1020
00:55:53.140 --> 00:55:55.240
It was an extremely exciting shoot.

1021
00:55:55.240 --> 00:56:04.320
And I kept telling the line producers afterwards, no matter what, you know, situation it was in, I was like, why can't we just jump in a van and go shoot it like, you know.

1022
00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:05.160
Like New York.

1023
00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:05.480
Yeah.

1024
00:56:05.580 --> 00:56:05.800
Yeah.

1025
00:56:05.860 --> 00:56:06.120
Yeah.

1026
00:56:06.260 --> 00:56:07.860
And just hop out really quick.

1027
00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:08.400
Yeah.

1028
00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:17.380
Well, I remember one year because the St. Patrick's Day parade is so famous in Scranton, there was always a lot of talk about us incorporating that parade into an episode.

1029
00:56:17.580 --> 00:56:21.840
There were so many pitches about someone being in the parade or whatever it was.

1030
00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:28.060
And I remember one year, Greg, you told me, you said, I think we're going to do it.

1031
00:56:28.620 --> 00:56:37.940
The problem is that, you know, we can't shoot the real parade because then the episode will come out after St. Patrick's Day.

1032
00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:39.880
So if we wanted to be the same –

1033
00:56:39.880 --> 00:56:42.240
Yeah, we'd have to shoot it like in August or something or –

1034
00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:47.640
And you told me that Scranton agreed to move their parade to August for us.

1035
00:56:48.040 --> 00:56:52.900
And I was like, the whole city is going to make a fake parade for us?

1036
00:56:53.000 --> 00:56:53.920
That's pretty amazing.

1037
00:56:54.260 --> 00:56:55.900
The city is super cool.

1038
00:56:56.240 --> 00:56:56.600
Yeah.

1039
00:56:56.600 --> 00:57:01.460
And actually, I'm still in touch with Tim Holmes and Michelle Dempsey.

1040
00:57:01.580 --> 00:57:01.900
Oh, yeah.

1041
00:57:01.900 --> 00:57:02.360
Remember them?

1042
00:57:02.560 --> 00:57:03.040
Of course.

1043
00:57:03.200 --> 00:57:09.120
And they're having a 20th anniversary exhibit in the Everhart Museum coming up.

1044
00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:11.020
Oh, that's fantastic.

1045
00:57:11.420 --> 00:57:12.280
Museum in Scranton.

1046
00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:12.740
Yeah.

1047
00:57:12.920 --> 00:57:15.560
We'll share that in our Instagram stories.

1048
00:57:15.880 --> 00:57:16.060
Yeah.

1049
00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:18.940
I'm not sure exactly when it's happening, but it's coming up soon.

1050
00:57:18.940 --> 00:57:21.960
I know we've been trying to get props and stuff to be sent out there.

1051
00:57:22.220 --> 00:57:23.620
What are they going to have there?

1052
00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:25.240
Have they gotten items?

1053
00:57:25.240 --> 00:57:26.000
Do you know?

1054
00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:30.160
Put them in touch with the NBC Prop Storage Group.

1055
00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:31.980
Like the desks and stuff?

1056
00:57:32.180 --> 00:57:34.140
Well, a lot of that stuff was sold.

1057
00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:34.880
Oh, right.

1058
00:57:34.940 --> 00:57:35.480
That's right.

1059
00:57:35.480 --> 00:57:36.020
That's right.

1060
00:57:36.020 --> 00:57:36.860
I did hear that.

1061
00:57:36.880 --> 00:57:38.020
You told us that, I think.

1062
00:57:38.140 --> 00:57:38.340
Yeah.

1063
00:57:38.580 --> 00:57:42.320
No, I was like, I'm going to hang on to all of the stuff.

1064
00:57:42.320 --> 00:57:44.640
And they were like, no, no, we have to save it for the Smithsonian.

1065
00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:48.940
And then I turned around and they were selling it on some auction place.

1066
00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:51.840
Well, it'd be cool to check out that exhibit.

1067
00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:53.840
I want to go online and look at it.

1068
00:57:54.080 --> 00:57:56.220
But, you know, I have Pam's desk.

1069
00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:57.260
You've seen that, right?

1070
00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:57.940
I'm sorry.

1071
00:57:58.060 --> 00:57:58.260
Wait.

1072
00:57:58.300 --> 00:57:58.560
What?

1073
00:57:58.600 --> 00:57:59.520
I'm going to start crying.

1074
00:57:59.740 --> 00:58:00.540
You have my desk?

1075
00:58:00.540 --> 00:58:04.380
I bought your reception desk online.

1076
00:58:04.980 --> 00:58:05.320
Wait.

1077
00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:06.560
Where is it?

1078
00:58:06.640 --> 00:58:07.200
You bought it in the auction?

1079
00:58:07.280 --> 00:58:08.280
I bought it in the auction.

1080
00:58:08.280 --> 00:58:08.620
Yeah.

1081
00:58:08.800 --> 00:58:09.900
It's in my offices.

1082
00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:11.640
No way.

1083
00:58:12.280 --> 00:58:13.680
Greg, can we come see it?

1084
00:58:13.820 --> 00:58:14.140
Yeah, for sure.

1085
00:58:14.140 --> 00:58:15.880
The reception desk for your offices?

1086
00:58:16.240 --> 00:58:22.240
No, it's just got, it's just sort of like on an area of its own with a little plaque on it.

1087
00:58:22.240 --> 00:58:23.380
You have to go sit in it, Jenna.

1088
00:58:24.060 --> 00:58:24.560
I don't.

1089
00:58:24.560 --> 00:58:26.020
Well, I don't know if you want to go sit in it.

1090
00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:26.520
I don't know.

1091
00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:27.160
It's so weird.

1092
00:58:27.320 --> 00:58:28.240
It's out of context.

1093
00:58:28.480 --> 00:58:32.280
I don't, it, oh my God, I don't know how that would be emotionally.

1094
00:58:32.640 --> 00:58:33.720
Yeah, it's extremely heavy.

1095
00:58:33.940 --> 00:58:39.520
I was, I'm considering sending it to the Everhart Museum for this exhibit, but.

1096
00:58:39.680 --> 00:58:42.320
You'd have to ship it like on a big truck or something.

1097
00:58:42.580 --> 00:58:43.320
I don't know, it might fall apart.

1098
00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:43.980
I don't know, I'm worried.

1099
00:58:43.980 --> 00:58:50.580
It was a real funky height, so it was really hard to get my legs underneath it.

1100
00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:54.660
There's like a shelf on the, sort of an awkward place on the back of it.

1101
00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:55.380
There is.

1102
00:58:55.500 --> 00:58:59.160
And also it was very narrow, like it's not deep.

1103
00:58:59.480 --> 00:59:03.260
So it only was as deep as a keyboard.

1104
00:59:03.940 --> 00:59:08.920
So if I ever had to pretend like I was writing something down, there was nowhere to write

1105
00:59:08.920 --> 00:59:11.320
it down or I'd have to like move the keyboard.

1106
00:59:11.760 --> 00:59:15.220
Well, I think it's, it's sort of like made for two levels, right?

1107
00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:19.900
Like if you wanted to type, you have a keyboard on a shelf that's sort of halfway down and

1108
00:59:19.900 --> 00:59:22.360
you can like lower the chair or something.

1109
00:59:22.940 --> 00:59:27.880
No, it was just like, there was just like a desk portion, but then there was the upper

1110
00:59:27.880 --> 00:59:31.920
portion where people would stand and Jim would rest his elbows.

1111
00:59:32.100 --> 00:59:32.660
Yeah, exactly.

1112
00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:33.440
Yeah.

1113
00:59:33.580 --> 00:59:37.780
So a lot of times in the show, if I'm writing a message or something and you see that I'm

1114
00:59:37.780 --> 00:59:42.440
doing it on that upper portion, it's because there was really barely any desk.

1115
00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:44.420
Well, that worked well for camera though.

1116
00:59:44.640 --> 00:59:45.240
It did.

1117
00:59:45.500 --> 00:59:45.700
Yeah.

1118
00:59:46.040 --> 00:59:50.140
No, it was a really well-made, you know, piece of furniture.

1119
00:59:50.320 --> 00:59:53.100
It was very iconic, fit the space perfectly.

1120
00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:54.800
It was unusual.

1121
00:59:55.400 --> 00:59:56.720
I want to come see it.

1122
00:59:56.780 --> 00:59:57.020
Yeah.

1123
00:59:57.220 --> 00:59:58.560
I can report back, Jenna.

1124
00:59:58.780 --> 00:59:59.360
I think on it.

1125
00:59:59.660 --> 01:00:00.060
All right.

1126
01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:01.760
I have another question for you, Greg.

1127
01:00:01.760 --> 01:00:07.280
Greg, if you could go back and relive a moment from your time on The Office, what would it

1128
01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:07.500
be?

1129
01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:08.880
Oh my goodness.

1130
01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:19.900
Well, I have to say that I loved, obviously, hanging out with the writers, but directing

1131
01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:27.920
was the most fun probably because of the cast and interacting with the caSt.

1132
01:00:27.920 --> 01:00:33.780
And I just, I don't know, the first thing that popped into my head was there was times

1133
01:00:33.780 --> 01:00:40.980
when I would direct Rain doing Talking Heads and we would get the giggles and just, you

1134
01:00:40.980 --> 01:00:43.720
know, it was super, super fun.

1135
01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:50.960
Because I also think that I have to say that Rain, you know, that Dwight is also another aspect

1136
01:00:50.960 --> 01:00:58.460
that I identify with strongly is the kind of weird survivalist nerd part, you know?

1137
01:00:58.680 --> 01:00:59.020
Yeah.

1138
01:01:00.740 --> 01:01:01.580
So, yeah.

1139
01:01:01.760 --> 01:01:08.320
So he and I would really kind of get into a groove going into that side of Dwight and

1140
01:01:08.320 --> 01:01:09.040
the Talking Heads.

1141
01:01:09.260 --> 01:01:12.000
And there's other characters I had to be, I couldn't direct.

1142
01:01:12.320 --> 01:01:17.900
Like for some reason, Creed would like have a meltdown if I was in the room directing.

1143
01:01:17.900 --> 01:01:22.780
So I would have to say to Creed, like, hey, I'm super busy.

1144
01:01:22.840 --> 01:01:24.020
I got to go use the bathroom.

1145
01:01:24.260 --> 01:01:26.200
You just keep going with the DP or whatever.

1146
01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:32.080
And I'd walk out and I'd listen to him so that he could do it without me staring at him.

1147
01:01:32.480 --> 01:01:35.040
I don't know what the issue was there.

1148
01:01:36.720 --> 01:01:39.080
Well, I love the bloopers so much.

1149
01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:41.040
And my kids love the bloopers.

1150
01:01:41.160 --> 01:01:42.400
So I've watched quite a lot of them.

1151
01:01:42.520 --> 01:01:47.420
And I would say the person that breaks the most is Rain and his Talking Heads.

1152
01:01:47.420 --> 01:01:47.700
Yes.

1153
01:01:47.840 --> 01:01:49.360
He's all over the bloopers.

1154
01:01:49.780 --> 01:01:56.040
His brain goes ahead of the material, like searching for alternates and stuff.

1155
01:01:56.200 --> 01:02:00.400
Because he knows he has the ability to go off script and do his own thing.

1156
01:02:00.540 --> 01:02:05.340
And so sometimes he would get ahead of the material and start cracking up about what he

1157
01:02:05.340 --> 01:02:08.300
could say that he knows is terribly wrong.

1158
01:02:08.600 --> 01:02:08.780
Yeah.

1159
01:02:09.120 --> 01:02:09.480
You know?

1160
01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:10.240
Yeah.

1161
01:02:10.240 --> 01:02:10.620
Yeah.

1162
01:02:11.500 --> 01:02:15.240
We also talked earlier about all of the auditions.

1163
01:02:15.540 --> 01:02:21.220
We went back in sort of honor of the 20th anniversary and we watched all these audition tapes.

1164
01:02:21.440 --> 01:02:22.360
Because they're online.

1165
01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:22.860
Yeah.

1166
01:02:23.000 --> 01:02:23.200
Yeah.

1167
01:02:23.300 --> 01:02:23.440
Yeah.

1168
01:02:23.440 --> 01:02:24.300
It's weird, isn't it?

1169
01:02:24.360 --> 01:02:25.460
That they're all out there.

1170
01:02:25.640 --> 01:02:25.900
I mean.

1171
01:02:26.120 --> 01:02:26.380
Yeah.

1172
01:02:26.500 --> 01:02:28.200
They're not the full audition.

1173
01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:28.760
You know?

1174
01:02:28.940 --> 01:02:29.000
No.

1175
01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:30.320
Tapes are just little clips.

1176
01:02:30.360 --> 01:02:30.920
No, I remember.

1177
01:02:30.920 --> 01:02:32.420
We had to get permission, I think.

1178
01:02:32.580 --> 01:02:34.360
They were a DVD extra.

1179
01:02:34.360 --> 01:02:35.440
Oh, I see.

1180
01:02:35.480 --> 01:02:37.700
And I think we had to get permission from everybody.

1181
01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:38.600
Yeah, that's right.

1182
01:02:38.600 --> 01:02:41.160
Because it's not cool to just like leak the audition tapes.

1183
01:02:41.460 --> 01:02:42.200
No, no, no.

1184
01:02:42.260 --> 01:02:42.640
You're right.

1185
01:02:42.800 --> 01:02:45.100
They're on, I forget what season it is.

1186
01:02:45.200 --> 01:02:49.860
But there's one DVD box set that has a whole bunch of extras.

1187
01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:52.880
It has like 21 minutes of bloopers.

1188
01:02:52.920 --> 01:02:57.960
And it has the, when everyone went to Scranton and it has cast interviews.

1189
01:02:57.960 --> 01:02:59.820
And I think it has audition tapes.

1190
01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:01.240
It's sort of a commemorative.

1191
01:03:01.240 --> 01:03:05.960
Well, they also did that sort of like retrospective before our finale.

1192
01:03:05.960 --> 01:03:08.840
And they put some of our audition tapes in there as well.

1193
01:03:09.100 --> 01:03:13.480
So do you remember that process of all of us auditioning?

1194
01:03:13.520 --> 01:03:13.720
Sure.

1195
01:03:13.880 --> 01:03:15.220
It was many months.

1196
01:03:15.460 --> 01:03:15.700
Yeah.

1197
01:03:15.900 --> 01:03:21.320
Well, the funny thing is where we're shooting the paper is across the street on the lot from

1198
01:03:21.320 --> 01:03:27.680
the little bungalow that Reveille was based at that we'd had all of our auditions at.

1199
01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:34.240
And I don't know if you remember this, but sitting in that bungalow hour after hour, it's really

1200
01:03:34.240 --> 01:03:37.620
funny because it's, the tram goes right past it.

1201
01:03:37.860 --> 01:03:38.080
Yes.

1202
01:03:38.180 --> 01:03:40.240
For the Universal Studios tour.

1203
01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:40.540
Yeah.

1204
01:03:40.620 --> 01:03:41.120
The tram.

1205
01:03:41.300 --> 01:03:42.460
The tram goes past it.

1206
01:03:42.560 --> 01:03:47.920
And you, you know, every like 12 minutes you hear someone go in and here is where they're

1207
01:03:47.920 --> 01:03:53.320
going to find the stars of tomorrow and we'll be sitting there looking at everybody.

1208
01:03:53.980 --> 01:03:59.880
No, I remember, well, I mean, I'm sure we've talked about this before, but your audition

1209
01:03:59.880 --> 01:04:07.260
was so extraordinary, Jenna, because it was like, wait a second, this is exactly Pam.

1210
01:04:07.520 --> 01:04:09.260
I don't understand what she's doing.

1211
01:04:09.580 --> 01:04:11.940
She's not, doesn't seem to really be doing anything.

1212
01:04:11.940 --> 01:04:17.800
And yet she's a hundred percent the character and a lot of, you know, everybody was trying

1213
01:04:17.800 --> 01:04:26.600
so hard in most auditions and you were almost like knowing, like not yes anding, you were

1214
01:04:26.600 --> 01:04:29.500
like negating all the suggestions for improv.

1215
01:04:29.680 --> 01:04:30.000
I was.

1216
01:04:30.120 --> 01:04:30.700
Do you remember that?

1217
01:04:30.920 --> 01:04:32.340
Yeah, it was a conscious decision.

1218
01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:34.020
Yeah, it was, it was brilliant.

