But I think that part of the bones of the show is that it is a documentary for real,

1462
01:16:34.780 --> 01:16:41.040
and it can tap into some of the things that make documentaries emotional.

1463
01:16:41.560 --> 01:16:43.680
And the finale was like that.

1464
01:16:43.820 --> 01:16:48.640
It was kind of like people summing their lives up and, you know.

1465
01:16:48.640 --> 01:16:49.240
Mm-hmm.

1466
01:16:49.340 --> 01:16:49.600
Yeah.

1467
01:16:49.800 --> 01:16:50.060
Yeah.

1468
01:16:50.400 --> 01:16:58.740
Because when you go back and watch the pilot in this superfan version that we watched was a Jim talking head that was extended that I hadn't seen.

1469
01:16:58.980 --> 01:16:59.280
Mm-hmm.

1470
01:16:59.640 --> 01:17:02.160
And that's one of the things in the rewatch.

1471
01:17:02.300 --> 01:17:07.800
There's so many talking heads I hadn't seen because we're not there when we film each other's talking heads, you know.

1472
01:17:08.020 --> 01:17:08.140
Right, right.

1473
01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:09.980
So there's all these kind of surprises.

1474
01:17:10.400 --> 01:17:14.300
And Jim says, well, let's see.

1475
01:17:14.520 --> 01:17:15.500
I'm not married.

1476
01:17:15.500 --> 01:17:16.680
I don't have kids.

1477
01:17:17.200 --> 01:17:19.140
But I was able to buy a car.

1478
01:17:19.280 --> 01:17:20.760
So I have a car.

1479
01:17:21.160 --> 01:17:23.860
And my car is five years old.

1480
01:17:24.880 --> 01:17:33.860
And I'm just like – then I think about like all the places that – where Jim goes, you know, and where he ends up and his life.

1481
01:17:33.860 --> 01:17:37.980
And I was just thinking about that, that that's exactly what you're talking about.

1482
01:17:38.080 --> 01:17:42.820
I felt like I had watched him become his fully realized self.

1483
01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:48.800
A lot of the extras for the pilot were stuff that I had added to the British script.

1484
01:17:48.980 --> 01:17:58.700
And then when we had to cut it down to time, a lot of those had to go because the original story had all the beats in it.

1485
01:17:58.780 --> 01:18:01.920
But a lot of those little grace notes were nice.

1486
01:18:01.920 --> 01:18:05.680
Well, the super fan episode is really satisfying to watch.

1487
01:18:05.800 --> 01:18:07.420
It's really, really fun to watch.

1488
01:18:07.540 --> 01:18:08.180
Oh, that's good.

1489
01:18:08.340 --> 01:18:18.560
And you get to feel that difference between the British pilot and then where you can know that our show is eventually going.

1490
01:18:18.560 --> 01:18:19.360
Is aiming at it.

1491
01:18:19.440 --> 01:18:19.880
Yeah, yeah.

1492
01:18:20.020 --> 01:18:20.340
It's cool.

1493
01:18:20.480 --> 01:18:23.460
I like that we've watched the whole original broadcaSt.

1494
01:18:23.520 --> 01:18:28.260
And now we get to see – it's like we got a dessert, you know.

1495
01:18:28.340 --> 01:18:28.600
Uh-huh.

1496
01:18:28.600 --> 01:18:29.840
And I appreciate it.

1497
01:18:29.840 --> 01:18:30.720
And then, you know,

1498
01:18:30.800 --> 01:18:43.480
we were talking to Dave Rogers about it and just the absolute care and integrity that he wants to make sure that these super fan episodes do not venture too far off the original mission.

1499
01:18:43.740 --> 01:18:46.460
And I'm just like they could not be in better hands.

1500
01:18:46.640 --> 01:18:48.840
And I know he's working directly with you on them.

1501
01:18:48.840 --> 01:18:54.060
And we're so thankful that Dave is sort of helping with all of that.

1502
01:18:54.240 --> 01:18:55.000
Oh, God, yeah.

1503
01:18:55.340 --> 01:18:59.300
Well, Dave is really the custodian of every moment of the show.

1504
01:18:59.600 --> 01:19:01.620
Oh, that's such a beautiful phrase.

1505
01:19:01.920 --> 01:19:02.160
Yeah.

1506
01:19:02.300 --> 01:19:02.460
Yeah.

1507
01:19:02.500 --> 01:19:04.360
He just knows everything about it.

1508
01:19:04.560 --> 01:19:04.820
Yeah.

1509
01:19:05.160 --> 01:19:08.560
As opposed to me who's like Mrs. 6.0 joke.

1510
01:19:09.880 --> 01:19:10.880
And Joe Bennett.

1511
01:19:11.960 --> 01:19:18.320
Well, before we move on to the final part of this interview, I know everybody's going to want us to ask.

1512
01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:20.360
You don't have to answer.

1513
01:19:20.840 --> 01:19:27.520
But we have to ask you about this news story that came out about a certain somebody who might be on the paper.

1514
01:19:28.180 --> 01:19:28.660
Yes.

1515
01:19:28.820 --> 01:19:32.520
A certain character from the office, Oscar.

1516
01:19:33.140 --> 01:19:33.420
Yes.

1517
01:19:33.520 --> 01:19:34.660
Well, we can now confirm.

1518
01:19:34.660 --> 01:19:39.900
You remember that when you visited the set, it was extreme secrecy.

1519
01:19:40.060 --> 01:19:41.880
Yes, he wasn't even called Oscar.

1520
01:19:42.140 --> 01:19:42.380
No.

1521
01:19:42.380 --> 01:19:43.800
Like on the script and stuff.

1522
01:19:44.260 --> 01:19:51.360
Well, my hope was that the first promo or the first piece of footage that it would be a surprise to the audience.

1523
01:19:51.360 --> 01:19:53.480
Like when Steve showed up in the finale?

1524
01:19:53.480 --> 01:19:54.000
Yes.

1525
01:19:54.000 --> 01:19:59.240
It's very difficult to keep a surprise nowadays with the amount of entertainment journalism.

1526
01:19:59.660 --> 01:20:00.880
And somebody sniffed it out.

1527
01:20:01.120 --> 01:20:01.220
How?

1528
01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:04.300
How did someone sniff it out?

1529
01:20:04.380 --> 01:20:05.000
I don't know.

1530
01:20:05.200 --> 01:20:16.780
But, you know, I mean, there was a lot of times where the tram would go by and Oscar would like dive behind a trash can or something.

1531
01:20:16.960 --> 01:20:25.860
When we were visiting, the tram went by and the NBC, the coordinator who had coordinated our visit, they were like, move away, move away from the tram.

1532
01:20:26.160 --> 01:20:28.580
Like they didn't want the tram to see us visiting.

1533
01:20:28.580 --> 01:20:30.020
And we dove behind a trailer.

1534
01:20:30.600 --> 01:20:37.860
And then a woman who just happened to be walking through the lot saw us and was like, oh, my gosh, and asked for our picture.

1535
01:20:38.100 --> 01:20:39.000
And we said yes.

1536
01:20:39.000 --> 01:20:39.720
We're like, sure.

1537
01:20:40.000 --> 01:20:43.720
And that NBC woman sprinted out and said, you have to, you can't post that.

1538
01:20:43.720 --> 01:20:44.400
You can't.

1539
01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:45.460
And we were like, oh.

1540
01:20:45.760 --> 01:20:47.480
And she was like, okay, you guys get in your cars.

1541
01:20:47.760 --> 01:20:48.380
Like, yeah.

1542
01:20:48.380 --> 01:20:57.180
Well, it's interesting because I always feel like it's more fun for surprises to hit while you're having the experience of watching the show.

1543
01:20:57.180 --> 01:21:11.580
And for an ordinary person who is going to watch the show several years later without paying attention to whatever the pre-launch publicity was, blogs and stuff,

1544
01:21:11.880 --> 01:21:13.040
is just going to be watching it.

1545
01:21:13.140 --> 01:21:18.100
So you want to build it so that the surprises are for the people watching the show.

1546
01:21:19.240 --> 01:21:24.620
But sometimes it's very difficult to promote things if you're going to try and keep a secret.

1547
01:21:24.980 --> 01:21:32.480
And so, for instance, I am also putting the finishing touches on the last season of Upload, season four.

1548
01:21:32.480 --> 01:21:43.020
And we ended season three on an enormous cliffhanger of which version of our lead character, Nathan, Robbie Amell.

1549
01:21:43.240 --> 01:21:46.500
He's, you know, a digital person who's been uploaded.

1550
01:21:46.720 --> 01:21:53.060
And at some point he was illegally copied and there's two of them in season three.

1551
01:21:53.060 --> 01:21:57.080
And then we sort of ended on this cliffhanger of one of them has been destroyed.

1552
01:21:57.080 --> 01:22:00.300
And anyway, so we were like, oh, cool.

1553
01:22:00.440 --> 01:22:04.480
It's going to be such an exciting beginning to season four when you find out which one.

1554
01:22:04.620 --> 01:22:11.280
And now I'm realizing, like, I don't know how to promote this because all of the, you know, any clip.

1555
01:22:11.380 --> 01:22:12.720
All of the footage is very clear.

1556
01:22:12.760 --> 01:22:15.860
It's going to make it pretty clear which one, you know, which one survived.

1557
01:22:16.040 --> 01:22:18.280
But I wasn't thinking along those lines.

1558
01:22:19.360 --> 01:22:31.020
But, yeah, so I was really trying to protect Oscar to be a fun surprise for people watching the new show to suddenly realize that he was a part of it.

1559
01:22:31.200 --> 01:22:37.440
And, you know, and the essential story of the show, I mean, Oscar is wonderful.

1560
01:22:37.500 --> 01:22:40.760
It's not the spinoff series for what happens to Oscar.

1561
01:22:40.760 --> 01:23:00.400
It's more the show of the documentary crew looking around for something else to do a documentary about and finding this newspaper that has fallen on hard times like most newspapers and does not even have a budget for any reporters.

1562
01:23:00.400 --> 01:23:12.840
So the idea is that a very, you know, idealistic person comes in to try and put this newspaper back on his feet and finds out that's Donald Gleason,

1563
01:23:13.080 --> 01:23:22.780
finds out that he doesn't have the budget to hire reporters and has to turn the existing staff of ad salespeople and, you know,

1564
01:23:22.980 --> 01:23:29.100
accountants and truck drivers into reporters to make some content for the paper.

1565
01:23:29.100 --> 01:23:33.280
And they don't know what they're doing and, you know, hilarity ensues.

1566
01:23:35.440 --> 01:23:37.500
Well, we're so excited to watch it.

1567
01:23:37.660 --> 01:23:39.520
Do you know when it's going to air?

1568
01:23:39.840 --> 01:23:41.040
Do you have an air date?

1569
01:23:42.440 --> 01:23:48.340
I think probably in the fall, like a back to school kind of a thing.

1570
01:23:48.360 --> 01:23:48.540
Okay.

1571
01:23:48.740 --> 01:23:50.360
Like it's like the old days.

1572
01:23:50.580 --> 01:23:54.620
Well, we can't wait to watch the paper and you'll let us know when we can start sharing about it.

1573
01:23:54.820 --> 01:23:55.120
Yes.

1574
01:23:55.600 --> 01:23:56.000
Of course.

1575
01:23:56.200 --> 01:23:56.320
Yes.

1576
01:23:56.480 --> 01:23:56.680
Okay.

1577
01:23:56.860 --> 01:23:57.160
Thank you.

1578
01:23:57.160 --> 01:24:00.440
So, Greg, we've started asking our guests the call sheet questions.

1579
01:24:00.580 --> 01:24:01.380
That's what we call them.

1580
01:24:01.660 --> 01:24:05.340
These were the questions on the back of our call sheets where we would get to know a crew member.

1581
01:24:05.580 --> 01:24:09.220
So we're going to ask you the office call sheet questions.

1582
01:24:09.420 --> 01:24:09.740
Okay.

1583
01:24:10.300 --> 01:24:10.620
Ready?

1584
01:24:10.920 --> 01:24:12.240
Question number one.

1585
01:24:12.900 --> 01:24:15.260
What was your first entertainment job?

1586
01:24:16.040 --> 01:24:16.800
Ah-ha-ha.

1587
01:24:18.960 --> 01:24:33.740
Well, I'll tell you that I track it from, it's not really a job, but my father worked for Capital Cities Broadcasting as his career when I was a kid.

1588
01:24:33.740 --> 01:24:35.460
And he worked at different radio stations.

1589
01:24:35.900 --> 01:24:43.560
And at one point, he was the manager of a rock radio station in Rhode Island.

1590
01:24:43.560 --> 01:24:51.760
And he used to go to this manager's meeting for all of the other station managers.

1591
01:24:52.090 --> 01:24:55.420
And he entertained at the meeting.

1592
01:24:55.420 --> 01:25:10.800
He had a Karnak-style act where he would put an envelope up to his head and read the answers and then open up the envelope and there would be the joke.

1593
01:25:10.800 --> 01:25:18.420
And so I wrote jokes for him for that act.

1594
01:25:18.420 --> 01:25:20.460
And we've used it in the show.

1595
01:25:20.780 --> 01:25:21.400
I remember that.

1596
01:25:21.400 --> 01:25:21.640
Do you remember this?

1597
01:25:21.940 --> 01:25:22.940
When Michael does it.

1598
01:25:22.960 --> 01:25:23.240
Yes.

1599
01:25:23.320 --> 01:25:24.260
And we used the same joke.

1600
01:25:24.400 --> 01:25:25.380
That was my first joke.

1601
01:25:26.000 --> 01:25:27.020
How old were you?

1602
01:25:27.100 --> 01:25:28.060
I was like a teenager.

1603
01:25:28.340 --> 01:25:30.180
And he did it for many years.

1604
01:25:30.180 --> 01:25:34.380
And eventually, various friends of mine wrote for him.

1605
01:25:34.380 --> 01:25:45.740
And at one point, he had Conan writing for him and this guy, Mike Reese, who was one of the showrunners of The Simpsons, who before – and obviously before, you know,

1606
01:25:46.260 --> 01:25:48.740
they had big careers.

1607
01:25:49.100 --> 01:25:57.400
So in your dad's, like, employee circle and just circle of friends, do they just think your dad is the funniest person they've ever met?

1608
01:25:57.680 --> 01:26:00.620
He had, like, the best comedy writers writing for him.

1609
01:26:00.620 --> 01:26:01.600
I've stolen a ton of stories.

1610
01:26:01.600 --> 01:26:06.660
My dad has, like, lived through a lot of funny things, and he turns them into very funny stories.

1611
01:26:06.660 --> 01:26:08.080
And I've used them.

1612
01:26:08.200 --> 01:26:17.340
So, for instance, I wrote an episode of Seinfeld that was 100% based on my dad where, you know, he lived in New York City and he had a car,

1613
01:26:17.380 --> 01:26:19.280
but it was too expensive to have a garage.

1614
01:26:19.280 --> 01:26:23.640
So every two days he would drive around looking for a parking space.

1615
01:26:23.800 --> 01:26:27.040
And sometimes it would take 45 minutes, an hour to find a parking space.

1616
01:26:27.040 --> 01:26:29.240
So at one point, he found one.

1617
01:26:29.240 --> 01:26:31.380
He was so excited, and he started to back into it.

1618
01:26:31.520 --> 01:26:33.860
And this other guy tried to front into it.

1619
01:26:34.060 --> 01:26:35.640
And neither of them would budge.

1620
01:26:35.640 --> 01:26:35.820
I remember that episode.

1621
01:26:35.820 --> 01:26:36.380
Do you remember this episode?

1622
01:26:36.620 --> 01:26:39.060
Yes, and it was like a standoff in the parking spot.

1623
01:26:39.140 --> 01:26:39.340
Yeah.

1624
01:26:39.520 --> 01:26:42.620
And this happened to him, and he wouldn't leave.

1625
01:26:42.700 --> 01:26:51.380
And a friend of his walked by, and he flagged him down, and he had him, you know, tell my mom to make him dinner and bring it to the parking spot.

1626
01:26:54.060 --> 01:26:59.800
And, you know, so I pitched that to Larry David and, you know, sold that as a freelance episode.

1627
01:27:00.220 --> 01:27:09.300
So I feel like I've taken enough from him where I'm happy to have given him, you know, jokes for his manager act.

1628
01:27:09.620 --> 01:27:10.560
I love that.

1629
01:27:10.720 --> 01:27:11.460
I love that so much.

1630
01:27:11.460 --> 01:27:11.980
So that would be my firSt.

1631
01:27:12.060 --> 01:27:13.340
I mean, that wasn't a paid job.

1632
01:27:13.340 --> 01:27:23.000
First paid job was Conan and I probably working for not necessarily the news back in the day.

1633
01:27:23.200 --> 01:27:24.320
As writing partners?

1634
01:27:24.480 --> 01:27:25.540
Yes, we were writing partners.

1635
01:27:25.860 --> 01:27:25.910
Yeah.

1636
01:27:26.680 --> 01:27:27.680
Next question.

1637
01:27:27.920 --> 01:27:31.820
Do you speak any other languages or play a musical instrument?

1638
01:27:32.240 --> 01:27:37.100
It's so funny you should bring that up because I am pounding away at Duolingo right now.

1639
01:27:37.260 --> 01:27:37.800
What language?

1640
01:27:37.800 --> 01:27:45.060
I'm doing Spanish and French and Portuguese and, like, Japanese.

1641
01:27:45.300 --> 01:27:46.220
I'm trying a whole bunch.

1642
01:27:46.420 --> 01:27:47.860
To see what sticks?

1643
01:27:48.280 --> 01:27:48.640
Yeah.

1644
01:27:48.760 --> 01:27:51.020
Well, sometimes you're, you know, mostly Spanish.

1645
01:27:51.380 --> 01:27:55.560
I'm making the most progress in Spanish, but sometimes I'll try some other ones.

1646
01:27:55.560 --> 01:28:06.660
And I just was at McCabe's Guitar Shop last week and I was listening to a concert and somebody was playing a mandolin.

1647
01:28:06.840 --> 01:28:07.860
And I was like, God damn it.

1648
01:28:07.880 --> 01:28:10.200
I got to learn how to play the mandolin before I die.

1649
01:28:10.480 --> 01:28:12.740
I really want to learn that.

1650
01:28:12.840 --> 01:28:14.980
But I have really no musical ability.

1651
01:28:15.260 --> 01:28:16.880
So Creed plays the mandolin.

1652
01:28:16.980 --> 01:28:17.240
Does he?

1653
01:28:17.380 --> 01:28:18.440
I'll bet he's great.

1654
01:28:18.980 --> 01:28:19.640
All right.

1655
01:28:19.760 --> 01:28:20.620
Next question.

1656
01:28:21.100 --> 01:28:24.340
What's a place you've been to that you absolutely loved?

1657
01:28:24.340 --> 01:28:30.740
Well, I'm, like, kind of, like, no matter where I go, I'm always like, could I live here?

1658
01:28:31.060 --> 01:28:31.940
This is cool.

1659
01:28:32.300 --> 01:28:36.160
And I remember driving out to L.A.

1660
01:28:36.820 --> 01:28:43.700
with Conan and another friend, Rob Lezevnik, to have jobs at not necessarily the news.

1661
01:28:43.700 --> 01:28:45.540
And we drove through Santa Fe, New Mexico.

1662
01:28:46.020 --> 01:28:49.420
And I remember being like, oh, my God, this place is magical.

1663
01:28:49.680 --> 01:28:50.680
How could I work here?

1664
01:28:50.680 --> 01:28:58.800
And the only job that I thought I might be able to work on was the newspaper because they had a newspaper there.

1665
01:28:59.000 --> 01:29:05.440
So I had a little fantasy of living in Santa Fe and working for whatever their newspaper was.

1666
01:29:05.640 --> 01:29:07.540
My in-laws live in Santa Fe.

1667
01:29:07.640 --> 01:29:07.880
Really?

1668
01:29:08.060 --> 01:29:08.240
Yeah.

1669
01:29:08.380 --> 01:29:10.920
Lee's dad and stepmom live in Santa Fe.

1670
01:29:11.080 --> 01:29:11.660
We go there all the time.

1671
01:29:11.660 --> 01:29:12.760
I love the smell of the air there.

1672
01:29:13.500 --> 01:29:17.680
Well, you know, it's very high elevation, actually.

1673
01:29:18.060 --> 01:29:19.220
Kind of sneaks up on you.

1674
01:29:19.340 --> 01:29:25.340
But we moved to Santa Fe during the pandemic to be closer to them, and that was our pod.

1675
01:29:25.980 --> 01:29:26.680
That's cool.

1676
01:29:27.040 --> 01:29:28.380
I mean, that's really cool.

1677
01:29:28.380 --> 01:29:28.840
It's great.

1678
01:29:29.100 --> 01:29:29.600
It's great.

1679
01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:30.360
Yeah.

1680
01:29:30.680 --> 01:29:30.920
Okay.

1681
01:29:31.460 --> 01:29:32.540
Question number four.

1682
01:29:32.540 --> 01:29:34.200
What do you like to do on the weekends?

1683
01:29:35.120 --> 01:29:40.320
Well, you know, I still have kids who can't drive.

1684
01:29:40.700 --> 01:29:42.940
My youngest is just learning how to drive.

1685
01:29:43.380 --> 01:29:48.660
And so my weekends are mostly driving her around.

1686
01:29:49.140 --> 01:29:49.400
Yeah.

1687
01:29:49.780 --> 01:29:50.500
I can relate.

1688
01:29:50.840 --> 01:29:51.460
I say something.

1689
01:29:51.560 --> 01:29:53.260
I'm just parent Uber, you know?

1690
01:29:53.980 --> 01:29:54.120
Yeah.

1691
01:29:54.480 --> 01:29:54.820
So.

1692
01:29:55.520 --> 01:29:57.400
That's what you do on the weekends.

1693
01:29:57.760 --> 01:29:58.000
Yeah.

1694
01:29:58.140 --> 01:29:59.940
What do you like to do on the weekends?

1695
01:30:00.060 --> 01:30:00.760
I don't need to.

